{"id": "103", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "The various systems in the human body work together to ensure its normal functioning. For instance, the urinary system works with all other systems to clean the blood by removing waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. This includes organs like the kidneys and lower intestines. Similarly, the respiratory system also collaborates with all other systems to provide oxygen to all body parts and remove carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. The respiratory system comprises the nose, larynx, trachea, and lungs.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the body systems work together by each system contributing to the overall functioning of the body. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. These systems are interconnected and rely on each other to maintain the body's normal functioning.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the body systems work together by each system contributing to the overall functioning of the body. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. These systems are interconnected and rely on each other to maintain the body's normal functioning.", "time_sec": 2.849, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 2487, 5942, 975, 3786, 553, 1817, 1849, 28720, 311, 279, 8084, 30201, 315, 279, 2487, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 279, 71635, 1849, 34194, 12291, 3871, 504, 279, 68297, 11, 51092, 11, 58971, 11, 41531, 11, 4225, 37120, 11, 323, 22596, 5942, 13, 576, 41531, 1849, 5707, 23552, 369, 678, 2487, 5479, 323, 28160, 12499, 39489, 504, 279, 51092, 11, 58971, 11, 68297, 11, 4225, 37120, 11, 71635, 11, 323, 22596, 5942, 13, 4220, 5942, 525, 82316, 323, 17188, 389, 1817, 1008, 311, 10306, 279, 2487, 594, 4622, 30201, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0332, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0133, -0.0003, -0.0416, -0.3297, -0.1633, -0.0061, -0.0024, -0.0135, -1.0133, -1.391, -0.2251, -1.0208, -0.1708, -0.378, -0.176, -0.5443, -0.0707, -0.0031, -0.0587, -0.2583, -0.6373, -0.2016, -0.098, -0.0063, -0.5936, -0.0099, -0.5695, -0.2615, -0.0087, -0.0078, -0.6665, -0.0049, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0018, -0.0001, -0.0716, -0.0, -0.1811, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.5634, -0.6693, -0.042, -0.0, -0.1682, -0.0009, -0.181, -0.0015, -0.0035, -0.0001, -0.0252, -0.0261, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0195, -0.2964, -0.0413, -0.0, -0.0029, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0078, -0.0088, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0314, -1.3035, -0.5778, -1.0791, -0.4067, -0.3662, -1.1669, -0.0003, -0.1036, -0.0002, -0.1203, -0.0844, -0.638, -0.1205, -0.0031, -0.2409, -0.5585, -0.0251, -0.0535], "cumulative_logprob": -18.1752, "norm_logp": -0.1874, "input_len": 390, "output_tokens": 97, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.16, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "105", "question": "how the body systems work together", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how the body systems work together:\n\n1. The skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, and nervous systems all work together to help keep the body functioning normally. (Passage 1)\n2. The urinary system works closely with all other systems of the body to clean blood from the body and remove waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. (Passage 2)\n3. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the body systems work together in the following ways:\n\n* The skeletal system provides support and structure for the body, while the muscular system helps move the body and its parts.\n* The digestive system breaks down food into nutrients that can be absorbed and used by the body, while the respiratory system provides oxygen for energy production.\n* The circulatory system delivers oxygenated blood to the body's cells and removes carbon dioxide, while the urinary system removes waste products from the blood.\n* The endocrine system produces hormones that regulate various bodily functions, including growth and development, metabolism, and reproductive processes.\n* The lymphatic system helps defend the body against infection and disease, while the immune system fights off foreign invaders.\n* The nervous system controls and coordinates the body's functions, including movement, sensation, and thought processes.\n\nOverall, the body's systems work together in a complex interplay to maintain homeostasis and ensure the proper functioning of the body.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the body systems work together by each system contributing to the overall functioning of the body. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. These systems are interconnected and rely on each other to maintain the body's normal functioning.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the body systems work together by each system contributing to the overall functioning of the body. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. The respiratory system provides oxygen for all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. These systems are interconnected and rely on each other to maintain the body's normal functioning.", "time_sec": 2.967, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 2487, 5942, 975, 3786, 553, 1817, 1849, 28720, 311, 279, 8084, 30201, 315, 279, 2487, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 279, 71635, 1849, 34194, 12291, 3871, 504, 279, 68297, 11, 51092, 11, 58971, 11, 41531, 11, 4225, 37120, 11, 323, 22596, 5942, 13, 576, 41531, 1849, 5707, 23552, 369, 678, 2487, 5479, 323, 28160, 12499, 39489, 504, 279, 51092, 11, 58971, 11, 68297, 11, 4225, 37120, 11, 71635, 11, 323, 22596, 5942, 13, 4220, 5942, 525, 82316, 323, 17188, 389, 1817, 1008, 311, 10306, 279, 2487, 594, 4622, 30201, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0332, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0133, -0.0003, -0.0416, -0.3297, -0.1633, -0.0061, -0.0024, -0.0135, -1.0133, -1.391, -0.2251, -1.0208, -0.1708, -0.378, -0.176, -0.5443, -0.0707, -0.0031, -0.0587, -0.2583, -0.6373, -0.2016, -0.098, -0.0063, -0.5936, -0.0099, -0.5695, -0.2615, -0.0087, -0.0078, -0.6665, -0.0049, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0018, -0.0001, -0.0716, -0.0, -0.1811, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.5634, -0.6693, -0.042, -0.0, -0.1682, -0.0009, -0.181, -0.0015, -0.0035, -0.0001, -0.0252, -0.0261, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0195, -0.2964, -0.0413, -0.0, -0.0029, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0078, -0.0088, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0314, -1.3035, -0.5778, -1.0791, -0.4067, -0.3662, -1.1669, -0.0003, -0.1036, -0.0002, -0.1203, -0.0844, -0.638, -0.1205, -0.0031, -0.2409, -0.5585, -0.0251, -0.0535], "cumulative_logprob": -18.1752, "norm_logp": -0.1874, "input_len": 390, "output_tokens": 97, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 37.66, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "107", "question": "what is the difference between the leeward and windward islands?", "gt": "The Windward and Leeward Islands are part of the Lesser Antilles of the Caribbean Sea. The Windward Islands are in the southeast and are exposed to the northeast trade winds, hence they receive more rain and are wetter. On the other hand, the Leeward Islands are located in the northwest and are away from the wind, making them drier.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between the leeward and windward islands is that the windward islands are exposed to the wind and receive more rain, making them wetter. The leeward islands, on the other hand, are away from the wind and receive less rain, making them drier.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between the leeward and windward islands is that the windward islands are exposed to the wind and receive more rain, making them wetter. The leeward islands, on the other hand, are away from the wind and receive less rain, making them drier.", "time_sec": 2.047, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 279, 512, 24035, 323, 9956, 1606, 29000, 374, 429, 279, 9956, 1606, 29000, 525, 14900, 311, 279, 9956, 323, 5258, 803, 11174, 11, 3259, 1105, 14401, 465, 13, 576, 512, 24035, 29000, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 525, 3123, 504, 279, 9956, 323, 5258, 2686, 11174, 11, 3259, 1105, 294, 7253, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.2675, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0095, -0.0005, -0.0643, -0.0067, -0.104, -0.0001, -0.003, -0.659, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0034, -0.0, -0.002, -0.1454, -0.2476, -0.2153, -0.1551, -0.0, -0.0982, -0.7563, -0.6998, -0.0003, -0.0037, -0.2168, -1.2427, -0.6349, -0.0062, -0.0512, -0.1515, -0.6385, -0.0128, -0.0114, -0.0012, -1.0148, -1.0618, -0.0785, -0.0, -0.0019, -0.4744, -0.2242, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0031, -0.8932, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0024, -0.