{"id": "197", "question": "leasing meaning", "gt": "Leasing refers to a process in which a firm obtains the use of fixed assets by making contractual, periodic, and tax-deductible payments to the owner of those assets. The lessee is the party receiving the services or assets under the lease contract, while the lessor is the owner of the assets.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed asset for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax deductible payments. The lessee is the receiver of the services or the assets under the lease contract and the lessor is the owner of the assets. The relationship between the tenant and the landlord is called a tenancy, and can be for a fixed or an indefinite period of time. The consideration for the lease is called rent.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, leasing is a process by which a firm can obtain the use of a certain fixed asset for which it must pay a series of contractual, periodic, tax deductible payments. The lessee is the receiver of the services or the assets under the lease contract and the lessor is the owner of the assets. The relationship between the tenant and the landlord is called a tenancy, and can be for a fixed or an indefinite period of time. The consideration for the lease is called rent.", "time_sec": 3.6, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 70319, 374, 264, 1882, 553, 892, 264, 7474, 646, 6851, 279, 990, 315, 264, 3654, 8356, 9329, 369, 892, 432, 1969, 2291, 264, 4013, 315, 75443, 11, 38345, 11, 3742, 77812, 14173, 13, 576, 3541, 4060, 374, 279, 13964, 315, 279, 3516, 476, 279, 11770, 1212, 279, 25064, 5116, 323, 279, 2686, 269, 374, 279, 6372, 315, 279, 11770, 13, 576, 5025, 1948, 279, 25239, 323, 279, 40147, 374, 2598, 264, 5779, 6572, 11, 323, 646, 387, 369, 264, 8356, 476, 458, 80856, 4168, 315, 882, 13, 576, 17879, 369, 279, 25064, 374, 2598, 8016, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.488, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0171, -0.0011, -0.0381, -0.0132, -0.2273, -0.0472, -0.1465, -0.2032, -0.0, -0.0024, -0.0036, -0.0424, -0.0063, -0.0004, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.6046, -0.0279, -0.0016, -0.1806, -0.189, -0.0511, -0.0009, -0.0017, -0.0042, -0.0021, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0027, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0036, -0.0383, -0.4347, -0.0006, -0.0087, -0.1644, -0.0591, -0.0, -0.2345, -0.0005, -0.0119, -0.0004, -0.0025, -0.0108, -0.0006, -0.1607, -0.0127, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.3153, -0.0009, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0044, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0265, -0.0191, -0.9605, -0.0936, -0.0052, -0.0013, -0.0622, -0.0042, -0.0037, -0.0013, -0.007, -0.0018, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0399, -0.0492, -0.0144, -0.0002, -0.0032, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0063, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0014, -0.0001, -0.0044, -0.116, -0.0466, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0007, -0.0006, -0.0283, -0.4098], "cumulative_logprob": -5.6839, "norm_logp": -0.0552, "input_len": 524, "output_tokens": 103, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.22, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "203", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main cause of necrotizing fasciitis is bacterial infection, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus. Additionally, other types of bacteria have also been associated with the disease. Trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, can also play a role in the development of necrotizing fasciitis. Surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures can also serve as a source of infection. Therefore, the answer is: Bacterial infection, including group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes) and Staphylococcus, and trauma.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. These bacteria can include group A Streptococcus (GAS), staphylococcus, and others. The infections caused by these bacteria are usually mild, but in rare cases, they can lead to a more dangerous infection that can destroy skin, fat, and the tissue covering the muscles within a very short time. This condition is sometimes referred to as flesh-eating bacteria.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. These bacteria can include group A Streptococcus (GAS), staphylococcus, and others. The infections caused by these bacteria are usually mild, but in rare cases, they can lead to a more dangerous infection that can destroy skin, fat, and the tissue covering the muscles within a very short time. This condition is sometimes referred to as flesh-eating bacteria.", "time_sec": 3.641, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 19053, 4640, 4849, 15195, 72, 19435, 374, 8881, 553, 3807, 12829, 315, 23157, 13, 4220, 23157, 646, 2924, 1874, 362, 35672, 417, 509, 91311, 320, 38, 1911, 701, 357, 1342, 88, 1074, 91311, 11, 323, 3800, 13, 576, 28920, 8881, 553, 1493, 23157, 525, 5990, 23034, 11, 714, 304, 8848, 5048, 11, 807, 646, 2990, 311, 264, 803, 11406, 18873, 429, 646, 6921, 6787, 11, 8664, 11, 323, 279, 19847, 18202, 279, 23648, 2878, 264, 1602, 2805, 882, 13, 1096, 2971, 374, 7025, 13862, 311, 438, 24955, 5655, 1095, 23157, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0774, -0.0, -0.0, -0.019, -0.0003, -0.0293, -0.1065, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0954, -0.4135, -0.0246, -0.2142, -0.1883, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.6644, -1.3057, -0.1518, -0.8844, -0.9338, -0.188, -0.0, -0.5927, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.1389, -0.0319, -0.0003, -0.2545, -0.4849, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0078, -0.0027, -0.0193, -0.4053, -0.0151, -1.4934, -0.7861, -0.0395, -0.0, -0.0035, -0.0003, -0.2838, -0.1075, -0.0021, -0.4662, -0.0424, -0.1995, -0.0053, -0.0021, -0.1479, -0.0029, -0.0003, -0.7008, -0.0, -0.1486, -0.3411, -0.0686, -0.796, -0.9349, -0.3691, -0.0674, -0.0077, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0499, -0.0009, -0.0005, -0.0111, -0.0, -0.103, -0.0002, -0.0234, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0292, -1.3115, -0.5202, -0.0464, -0.5049, -0.307, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4803, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -1.0007, -0.4333], "cumulative_logprob": -19.0927, "norm_logp": -0.1948, "input_len": 668, "output_tokens": 98, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 21.82, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "205", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Chard is a vegetable that has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked or sautéed. Chard is in the same subspecies as beetroot and is cultivated for leaf production. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stems are slightly tougher. Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator for a minimum amount of time.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "time_sec": 4.258, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 48012, 2997, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 11, 773, 432, 1265, 387, 9768, 304, 279, 44944, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0681, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0174, -0.0012, -0.0415, -0.204, -0.0, -0.0701, -0.0227, -0.9447, -0.0642, -0.1047, -0.0056, -0.5632, -0.4992, -1.4555, -1.1862, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.4229, -0.0406, -0.3099, -0.1194, -0.5451, -0.0941, -0.0104, -0.1741, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4813, -0.0, -0.0244, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0119, -0.0016, -0.0069, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0322, -0.293, -0.0, -0.179, -0.2398, -0.0206, -0.0002, -0.4348, -0.1302, -0.0002, -0.0026, -0.0195, -0.0019, -0.0177, -0.0, -0.6112, -0.0649, -0.0289, -0.116, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0905, -0.0359, -0.5791, -0.0228, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0108, -0.0005, -0.0043, -0.0048, -0.0018, -0.0, -0.0692, -0.0327, -0.0099, -0.0628, -0.0, -0.0171, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0027, -0.0033, -0.0004, -0.8146, -0.3142, -0.0, -0.5369, -0.9925, -0.0, -0.0, -0.5816, -0.0249, -0.2222, -0.3129, -0.0042, -0.5362, -0.6343, -0.1264, -0.1309, -0.2053, -1.056, -0.0001, -0.1617, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4458, -0.1464], "cumulative_logprob": -17.8845, "norm_logp": -0.1611, "input_len": 571, "output_tokens": 111, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 68.38, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "207", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is what can be inferred about the vegetable \"chard\":\n\n* Chard is a type of beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) that has been chosen for leaf production over storage root formation.\n* Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\n* The flavor of chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive taste (some consider it bitter).\n* Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce bitterness and bring out a refined flavor.\n* Chard is an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden.\n* Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator to minimize spoilage.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What is chard vegetable?\" is: A beet variety (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) grown for leaf production, characterized by large, fleshy leaves and stalks with a strong, bitter flavor that becomes more refined with cooking.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "time_sec": 3.928, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 48012, 2997, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 13, 29160, 3908, 521, 567, 646, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 13, 910, 567, 374, 9016, 82117, 480, 11, 773, 432, 1265, 387, 9768, 304, 279, 44944, 369, 220, 17, 311, 220, 18, 2849, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0681, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0174, -0.0012, -0.0415, -0.204, -0.0, -0.0701, -0.0227, -0.9447, -0.0642, -0.1047, -0.0056, -0.5632, -0.4992, -1.4555, -1.1862, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.4229, -0.0406, -0.3099, -0.1194, -0.5451, -0.0941, -0.0104, -0.1741, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4813, -0.0, -0.0244, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0119, -0.0016, -0.0069, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0322, -0.293, -0.0, -0.179, -0.2398, -0.0206, -0.0002, -0.4348, -0.1302, -0.0002, -0.0026, -0.0195, -0.0019, -0.0177, -0.0, -0.6112, -0.0649, -0.0289, -0.116, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0905, -0.0359, -0.5791, -0.0228, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0108, -0.0005, -0.0043, -0.0048, -0.0018, -0.0, -0.0692, -0.0327, -0.0099, -0.0628, -0.0, -0.0171, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0027, -0.0033, -0.0004, -0.8146, -0.3142, -0.0, -0.5369, -0.9925, -0.0, -0.0, -0.5816, -0.0249, -0.2222, -0.3129, -0.0042, -0.5362, -0.6343, -0.1264, -0.1309, -0.2053, -1.056, -0.0001, -0.1617, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4458, -0.1464], "cumulative_logprob": -17.8845, "norm_logp": -0.1611, "input_len": 571, "output_tokens": 111, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.88, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "210", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom, meaning all the atoms in an element have the same number of protons. Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and so retain their own identity. The individual components of a mixture can be physically separated from each other. Mixtures have variable compositions and show the properties of their constituent substances. Elements and compounds just mix together to form a mixture and no new compound is formed.", "pred": "1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\n\n1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "time_sec": 5.181, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 34157, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 323, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 624, 17, 13, 13317, 3095, 525, 32055, 23415, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 323, 525, 1083, 10526, 32055, 13, 576, 5424, 304, 264, 23628, 525, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 4157, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 624, 18, 13, 19219, 18513, 525, 264, 6961, 10601, 315, 5424, 476, 31146, 1380, 279, 5479, 315, 279, 20980, 525, 537, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 646, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 13, 19219, 18513, 646, 614, 264, 3890, 18037, 323, 1473, 279, 5888, 315, 862, 74064, 32055, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1444, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0118, -0.0005, -0.1424, -0.4201, -0.1473, -0.0008, -0.0024, -0.002, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2343, -0.2807, -0.0001, -0.0919, -0.3319, -0.0126, -0.2876, -0.7568, -1.0507, -0.0063, -0.1071, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.002, -0.7509, -0.1401, -0.0908, -0.0137, -0.0001, -0.0029, -0.0004, -0.0221, -0.0029, -0.0001, -0.4791, -0.0079, -0.0, -0.0317, -0.0, -0.0089, -0.1718, -0.0473, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.001, -0.5923, -0.021, -0.6132, -0.0223, -0.0008, -0.4598, -0.7456, -0.5915, -0.2004, -0.049, -0.0, -0.0096, -0.0427, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.7492, -1.025, -0.0061, -0.1245, -0.231, -0.0431, -0.0433, -0.1158, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0027, -0.0, -0.0589, -0.5763, -0.0705, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0042, -0.0026, -0.0491, -0.0003, -0.0251, -0.3856, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0139, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0596, -0.824, -0.7021, -0.1326, -0.0382, -0.0008, -0.1607, -0.3983, -0.2245, -0.0001, -0.73, -0.5372, -0.2131, -0.0049, -0.0, -0.0681, -0.1998, -0.0087, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.998, -0.0037, -0.002, -0.4344, -0.298], "cumulative_logprob": -18.7242, "norm_logp": -0.1486, "input_len": 563, "output_tokens": 126, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.15, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "212", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Here are my answers to the question based on the provided passages:\n\nQ: What are the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures?\nA: Based on the passages provided, here are the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures:\n\nElements:\n\n* Are the purest form of a chemical substance (Passage 3)\n* Consist of only one type of atom with the same number of protons (Passage 3)\n\nCompounds:\n\n* Are substances composed of two or more different elements (Passage 3)\n* Are also pure substances (Passage 3)\n\nMixtures:\n\n* Contain two or more substances that are relatively easy to separate (Passage 1)\n* Have a variable composition (Passage 2)\n* Show the properties of their constituent substances (Passage 2)\n* Can be separated by physical means (Passage 2)", "pred": "1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\n\n1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of el