{"id": "227", "question": "how to fix lift valve in toilet", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Here's a brief answer on how to fix a lift valve in a toilet based on the given passages:\n\nTo fix a lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\n\n1. Shut off the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove any remaining broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n3. Install a new flapper valve and chain for a 1.6 gallon per flush toilet.\n4. Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\n5. Adjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links if necessary. Cut off any excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\n6. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included, screw it in tightly.\n7. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm, which is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use pliers to ensure it is securely fastened.\n8. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "pred": "1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms.\n3. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n4. Open the water valve to fill the tank.\n5. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\n6. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\n7. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\n8. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle.\n9. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly.\n10. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure.\n11. The other end will hang freely in the air.\n12. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm.\n13. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm.\n14. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\n15. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\n16. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to fix the lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\n\n1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms.\n3. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n4. Open the water valve to fill the tank.\n5. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\n6. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\n7. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\n8. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle.\n9. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly.\n10. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure.\n11. The other end will hang freely in the air.\n12. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm.\n13. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm.\n14. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\n15. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\n16. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "time_sec": 10.591, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 5046, 279, 11893, 31430, 304, 264, 26214, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1417, 27440, 279, 3015, 8149, 31430, 311, 279, 26214, 624, 17, 13, 10783, 279, 2310, 1320, 3106, 31430, 553, 63702, 432, 1007, 279, 33639, 11715, 624, 18, 13, 10783, 1128, 748, 2115, 315, 279, 10865, 11893, 8781, 504, 279