{"id": "242", "question": "how do crab traps work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how crab traps work:\n\nCrab traps are made of a metal cage with a system of nets inside, which are designed to allow crabs to enter but not escape. The traps have two leads that angle into the pot from the sides and bottom, leading to a narrow slit entrance. The crabs crawl up the leads to reach the bait inside the pot, but cannot exit through the slit. The pot is designed to keep the crabs inside until they can be retrieved by the fisherman.\n\nAdditionally, according to passage 3, crab pots can be equipped with mechanisms that disable them over time, preventing them from continuing to catch crabs after they have been abandoned or become lost. These mechanisms can include biodegradable mesh or dissolvable cords.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. The crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. The crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.", "time_sec": 4.237, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 59412, 44560, 975, 553, 1667, 264, 1849, 315, 52457, 4766, 264, 3460, 9317, 35360, 1865, 315, 9067, 476, 10063, 9317, 11294, 429, 374, 48580, 448, 9509, 15904, 13, 576, 44560, 525, 6188, 311, 2138, 1560, 3435, 304, 714, 537, 1077, 1105, 700, 13, 576, 59412, 3338, 374, 264, 3460, 9334, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1550, 323, 220, 23, 7541, 6884, 13, 9043, 10335, 614, 2990, 21853, 429, 9210, 1119, 279, 22862, 504, 279, 11067, 323, 5622, 311, 264, 15026, 78030, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1119, 279, 3338, 13, 1096, 78030, 374, 279, 1172, 19809, 1119, 279, 22862, 13, 576, 1560, 3435, 13083, 807, 1969, 45664, 705, 279, 11508, 311, 633, 311, 862, 1910, 15145, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0344, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0159, -0.0012, -0.0715, -0.0229, -0.0167, -0.1003, -0.1398, -0.3201, -0.0317, -0.1357, -0.0159, -0.0061, -0.0079, -0.0114, -0.0138, -0.0074, -0.0002, -0.2648, -0.0025, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.3352, -0.0015, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0188, -0.2337, -0.5133, -0.468, -0.055, -0.016, -0.0081, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.1314, -0.0082, -0.0036, -0.0048, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0841, -0.8743, -0.9532, -0.5617, -0.0921, -0.0873, -0.0035, -0.0025, -0.1191, -0.0023, -0.0072, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0021, -0.0009, -0.0029, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.6367, -0.0304, -0.0004, -0.0265, -0.013, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0069, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0033, -0.0555, -0.0641, -0.0048, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0515, -0.0623, -0.0076, -0.0, -0.0909, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.0009, -0.0057, -0.0036, -0.0001, -0.0494, -0.1651], "cumulative_logprob": -7.1105, "norm_logp": -0.0583, "input_len": 598, "output_tokens": 122, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 45.4, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 