{"id": "246", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between the Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA) and the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) are:\n\n1. Scope of application: The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\n2. Definition of disability: The FEHA requires that a disability must limit a major life activity, while the ADA requires that a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.\n3. Number of employees: The ADA applies to employers with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA has no such requirement.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between FEHA and ADA is the scope of application and the definition of disability.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between FEHA and ADA is that FEHA applies to more employees and doesn't distinguish between private and public employers, whereas ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees. Additionally, FEHA requires a disability, while ADA requires a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits a major life activity.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between FEHA and ADA is that FEHA applies to more employees and doesn't distinguish between private and public employers, whereas ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees. Additionally, FEHA requires a disability, while ADA requires a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits a major life activity.", "time_sec": 2.336, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 27931, 17020, 323, 78325, 374, 429, 27931, 17020, 