{"id": "361", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "The main difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy is the timing of the attack. Ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making the argument instead of attacking the argument directly, but poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive attack on a person's character or integrity before they have even made their argument. In other words, ad hominem fallacy is a reaction to an argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive strike to discredit an opponent before they have a chance to present their argument.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties. The key difference is that the ad hominem fallacy occurs during the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens before the argument begins.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument rather than attacking the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties. The key difference is that the ad hominem fallacy occurs during the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy happens before the argument begins.", "time_sec": 3.898, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 389, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 429, 4990, 1992, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 11, 6423, 287, 279, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 13, 576, 1376, 6672, 374, 429, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 13666, 2337, 279, 5693, 11, 1393, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 8573, 1573, 279, 5693, 12033, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.2207, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0241, -0.0007, -0.108, -0.1001, -0.6706, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0029, -0.0, -0.4839, -0.1915, -0.1039, -0.1135, -0.0961, -0.0114, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0398, -0.2847, -0.0003, -0.2499, -0.0041, -0.0055, -0.4757, -0.0002, -0.6984, -0.0, -0.8635, -0.0001, -0.0009, -0.6943, -0.272, -1.1303, -0.1863, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.6903, -0.4917, -0.0028, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0078, -0.512, -0.0161, -0.0001, -0.4743, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.2057, -0.6212, -0.0001, -0.0036, -0.0235, -0.0062, -0.0002, -0.0512, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0033, -0.2108, -0.109, -0.0, -0.2845, -0.0009, -0.0331, -0.0238, -0.0485, -0.0001, -0.0193, -0.0234, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0636, -0.657, -0.7761, -0.0119, -0.4484, -0.1539, -0.5034, -0.079, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.1543, -0.0001, -0.9335, -0.133, -0.1763, -0.2971, -0.2925, -0.1167, -0.0214, -0.0023, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0054, -0.0, -0.4915, -0.6757, -0.0287, -0.3584, -1.0725, -0.25, -0.0446], "cumulative_logprob": -18.6493, "norm_logp": -0.1695, "input_len": 392, "output_tokens": 110, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.33, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "367", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust that is normally exposed on the surface or underlying the soil. On the other hand, a stone is described as a smaller piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. The passages also mention that rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, while rocks cannot. Therefore, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size, with rocks being larger and stones being smaller and easier to handle.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, normally exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone is a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but one cannot do so with rocks.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is that a rock is the hard material of the earth's crust, normally exposed on the surface of the earth or sometimes underlying the soil, while a stone is a small piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, but one cannot do so with rocks.", "time_sec": 3.355, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 264, 6946, 323, 264, 9798, 374, 429, 264, 6946, 374, 279, 2588, 3684, 315, 279, 9393, 594, 39612, 11, 14279, 14900, 389, 279, 7329, 315, 279, 9393, 476, 7025, 16533, 279, 17171, 11, 1393, 264, 9798, 374, 264, 2613, 6573, 476, 1051, 59880, 429, 646, 387, 11691, 304, 279,