{"id": "438", "question": "how to find someone in france", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the steps to find someone in France:\n\n1. Check the French White Pages (in English): You can use the website pagesjaunes.fr to search for people in France. To search for someone in Paris or its suburbs, enter \"IDF\" (Ile de France) in the department or region field instead of typing Paris.\n2. Use the nom field to enter the last name of the person you are looking for, and you can also include their first name in the prenom field.\n3. Provide address information, if known, in the ou section to refine your search.\n4. Click \"trouver\" to search for the name, and the search results will display name, address, and phone number for all listings that match your search criteria.\n\nNote that the effectiveness of these methods may depend on how up-to-date the information in the white pages is and whether the person you are looking for has listed their contact information publicly.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (in English) at http://www.pagesjaunes.fr/pb.cgi?lang=en. When searching, instead of typing Paris in the City field, write IDF (or Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field. This will search for the person in Paris and its suburbs. Additionally, you can try posting an MC (missed connection) on Paris Craigslist (http://paris.craigslist.org/cgi-bin/pers...", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to find someone in France, you can use the French white pages (in English) at http://www.pagesjaunes.fr/pb.cgi?lang=en. When searching, instead of typing Paris in the City field, write IDF (or Ile de France) in the Dept district or region field. This will search for the person in Paris and its suburbs. Additionally, you can try posting an MC (missed connection) on Paris Craigslist (http://paris.craigslist.org/cgi-bin/pers...", "time_sec": 3.514, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 1477, 4325, 304, 9625, 11, 498, 646, 990, 279, 8585, 4158, 6816, 320, 258, 6364, 8, 518, 1758, 1110, 2136, 37980, 5580, 8531, 18798, 4322, 65, 59014, 30, 5205, 61757, 13, 3197, 15039, 11, 4518, 315, 19496, 12095, 304, 279, 4311, 2070, 11, 3270, 71555, 320, 269, 358, 273, 409, 9625, 8, 304, 279, 50221, 9290, 476, 5537, 2070, 13, 1096, 686, 2711, 369, 279, 1697, 304, 12095, 323, 1181, 46913, 13, 22406, 11, 498, 646, 1430, 17004, 458, 20869, 320, 1831, 291, 3633, 8, 389, 12095, 72292, 320, 1254, 1110, 1732, 285, 520, 956, 48922, 2659, 70416, 49125, 4322, 388, 1112, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.2603, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0444, -0.0005, -0.06, -0.7088, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.2461, -0.2329, -0.0132, -1.0185, -0.2977, -0.4982, -0.1564, -0.0007, -0.7197, -0.5392, -0.0029, -0.1569, -0.6934, -0.3039, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.015, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0297, -1.6464, -0.6107, -0.1231, -0.504, -0.0, -0.0054, -0.7862, -0.0073, -0.0001, -0.0077, -0.0, -0.0012, -0.0551, -0.0255, -0.0091, -0.0513, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0026, -0.0027, -0.0001, -0.0156, -0.0097, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3091, -0.0379, -0.0299, -0.1035, -0.0016, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0018, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0369, -0.985, -0.0002, -0.105, -0.0068, -0.2392, -0.0441, -0.1852, -0.0188, -0.0014, -0.0085, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.006, -0.0008, -0.7628, -0.0061, -0.0001, -0.001, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0005, 0.0, -0.0003, -0.0572, -0.0074, -0.0015, -0.0, -0.272, -0.4686], "cumulative_logprob": -13.5691, "norm_logp": -0.1234, "input_len": 648, "output_tokens": 110, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.22, "GAVIE-Rel": 5.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 3.3, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "440", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "gt": "Forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines because they help in the regeneration of the forest. Fires consume dead and decaying vegetation, clearing the way for new growth. Jack pines rely on fire to spread their seeds. They produce seratonous (resin-filled) cones that remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin, causing the cones to open and the seeds to fall or blow out. Furthermore, fires can disrupt the growth of other trees like spruce, providing an advantage to the more fire-tolerant jack pines and allowing them to proliferate.", "pred": "1. Fire consumes dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, clearing the way for new growth.\n2. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonous (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out.\n3. In the Canadian boreal forest, aspen and jack pine are the most important 'pioneer species'. These species can regenerate after a fire, as they are more fire-tolerant and can begin to proliferate again after another fire comes and disturbs the cycle.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines in several ways:\n\n1. Fire consumes dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, clearing the way for new growth.\n2. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonous (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out.\n3. In the Canadian boreal forest, aspen and jack pine are the most important 'pioneer species'. These species can regenerate after a fire, as they are more fire-tolerant and can begin to proliferate again after another fire comes and disturbs the cycle.", "time_sec": 5.886, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 13638, 26084, 525, 23699, 311, 390, 98813, 1075, 25072, 281, 1543, 304, 3807, 5510, 1447, 16, 13, 6647, 59506, 5593, 11, 1622, 17270, 53732, 389, 279, 13638, 6422, 11, 32750, 279, 1616, 369, 501, 6513, 624, 17, 13, 4329, 9419, 11, 1741, 438, 279, 25072, 33597, 11, 17188, 389, 3940, 311, 8865, 862, 19056, 13, 576, 25072, 33597, 18644, 1420, 23535, 782, 320, 416, 258, 43418, 8, 86892, 429, 525, 1602, 26128, 13, 576, 86892, 7146, 88360, 3080, 264, 3940, 13666, 323, 98091, 279, 53514, 13, 5005, 279, 86892, 2420, 1787, 323, 279, 19056, 4399, 476, 12244, 700, 624, 18, 13, 758, 279, 11888, 38686, 278, 13638, 11, 438, 2752, 323, 25072, 33597, 525, 279, 1429, 2989, 364, 79, 55398, 9419, 4427, 4220, 9419, 646, 79451, 1283, 264, 3940, 11, 438, 807, 525, 803, 3940, 2385, 22072, 517, 323, 646, 3161, 311, 41936, 349, 1549, 1283, 2441, 3940, 4041, 323, 25343, 1279, 279, 10775, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1957, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0197, -0.0014, -0.076, -0.0185, -0.0002, -0.1632, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0044, -0.0012, -0.0041, -0.0225, -0.0054, -0.0, -0.2964, -0.5098, -0.0016, -0.4729, -0.0173, -0.0003, -0.74, -1.6153, -0.0043, -0.0369, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.8875, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0078, -0.3064, -0.0812, -0.021, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0017, -0.8979, -0.0034, -0.0, -1.3685, -0.3992, -0.117, -0.1997, -0.0, -0.1447, -0.0004, -0.001, -0.0047, -0.0593, -0.0001, -0.0031, -0.0321, -0.0153, -0.0014, -0.0003, -0.1496, -0.292, -0.2432, -0.0002, -0.1712, -0.032, -0.0614, -0.0016, -0.5781, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0135, -0.5631, -0.0658, -0.0, -0.2268, -0.5146, -0.0011, -0.0018, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0024, -0.0001, -0.0195, -0.0276, -0.0053, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3984, -0.0291, -0.0233, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.079, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0