{"id": "444", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines because they help to clear away dead vegetation and promote new growth. According to passage 2, the jack pine produces serotonous cones that are dormant until a fire occurs, at which point they melt and release their seeds. This suggests that fire is a mechanism for the dispersal of jack pine seeds, and that the tree relies on fires to spread its seeds. Additionally, passage 3 suggests that while spruce trees may outcompete jack pine and aspen in some areas, fires can disrupt this cycle and allow jack pine and aspen to regain an advantage. Therefore, it seems that fires play a role in promoting the growth and survival of jack pine and other pioneer species in the boreal forest.", "pred": "1. Fire consumes dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, clearing the way for new growth.\n2. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonous (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out.\n3. In the Canadian boreal forest, aspen and jack pine are the most important 'pioneer species'. These species can regenerate after a fire, as they are more fire-tolerant and can begin to proliferate again after another fire comes and disturbs the cycle.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines in several ways:\n\n1. Fire consumes dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, clearing the way for new growth.\n2. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonous (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out.\n3. In the Canadian boreal forest, aspen and jack pine are the most important 'pioneer species'. These species can regenerate after a fire, as they are more fire-tolerant and can begin to proliferate again after another fire comes and disturbs the cycle.", "time_sec": 5.85, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 13638, 26084, 525, 23699, 311, 390, 98813, 1075, 25072, 281, 1543, 304, 3807, 5510, 1447, 16, 13, 6647, 59506, 5593, 11, 1622, 17270, 53732, 389, 279, 13638, 6422, 11, 32750, 279, 1616, 369, 501, 6513, 624, 17, 13, 4329, 9419, 11, 1741, 438, 279, 25072, 33597, 11, 17188, 389, 3940, 311, 8865, 862, 19056, 13, 576, 25072, 33597, 18644, 1420, 23535, 782, 320, 416, 258, 43418, 8, 86892, 429, 525, 1602, 26128, 13, 576, 86892, 7146, 88360, 3080, 264, 3940, 13666, 323, 98091, 279, 53514, 13, 5005, 279, 86892, 2420, 1787, 323, 279, 19056, 4399, 476, 12244, 700, 624, 18, 13, 758, 279, 11888, 38686, 278, 13638, 11, 438, 2752, 323, 25072, 33597, 525, 279, 1429, 2989, 364, 79, 55398, 9419, 4427, 4220, 9419, 646, 79451, 1283, 264, 3940, 11, 438, 807, 525, 803, 3940, 2385, 22072, 517, 323, 646, 3161, 311, 41936, 349, 1549, 1283, 2441, 3940, 4041, 323, 25343, 1279, 279, 10775, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1957, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0197, -0.0014, -0.076, -0.0185, -0.0002, -0.1632, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0044, -0.0012, -0.0041, -0.0225, -0.0054, -0.0, -0.2964, -0.5098, -0.0016, -0.4729, -0.0173, -0.0003, -0.74, -1.6153, -0.0043, -0.0369, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.8875, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0078, -0.3064, -0.0812, -0.021, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0017, -0.8979, -0.0034, -0.0, -1.3685, -0.3992, -0.117, -0.1997, -0.0, -0.1447, -0.0004, -0.001, -0.0047, -0.0593, -0.0001, -0.0031, -0.0321, -0.0153, -0.0014, -0.0003, -0.1496, -0.292, -0.2432, -0.0002, -0.1712, -0.032, -0.0614, -0.0016, -0.5781, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.00