{"id": "516", "question": "history of the word gemini", "gt": "Sure! Here's a brief history of the word Gemini based on the given passages:\n\nThe word \"Gemini\" comes from Latin, meaning \"twins.\" The constellation Gemini was first mentioned in Aristotle's Meteorologica over 300 years BC. In Greek mythology, the twins represented in Gemini are Castor and Pollux, who share the same mother, Leda, but have different fathers. The constellation Gemini is not in the same position as the constellation of Gemini and is a mutable sign that announces change and rules the time of year when Taurus spring has ended.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you need further clarification or if there's anything else I can help you with.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins and it is one of the constellations that actually look like its name would suggest. The first known reference of the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC. He mentions that he observed an occulting a star in Gemini and speaks of observing Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "time_sec": 2.643, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 3409, 330, 95040, 6591, 1, 374, 264, 19458, 3409, 369, 43636, 323, 432, 374, 825, 315, 279, 733, 613, 804, 429, 3520, 1401, 1075, 1181, 829, 1035, 4190, 13, 576, 1156, 3881, 5785, 315, 279, 85711, 82386, 572, 304, 77901, 748, 39632, 1609, 3001, 11, 916, 220, 18, 15, 15, 1635, 18040, 13, 1260, 33845, 429, 566, 13166, 458, 73826, 287, 264, 6774, 304, 85711, 323, 21191, 315, 44971, 49689, 304, 31446, 448, 432, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1448, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0111, -0.0017, -0.0992, -0.0511, -0.6829, -0.0357, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.0026, -0.8518, -0.2209, -0.0008, -0.1283, -0.1836, -0.0154, -0.3956, -0.9197, -0.1029, -0.0216, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0017, -0.0, -0.0, -0.013, -0.111, -0.0246, -0.0001, -0.0018, -0.0001, -0.0058, -0.0002, -0.0119, -0.0271, -0.015, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0186, -0.0006, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0029, -0.005, -0.0008, -0.4294, -0.0028, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0165, -0.0045, -0.0002, -0.001, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0383, -0.1957, -0.0052, -0.0033, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0927, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0037, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0406, -0.1056], "cumulative_logprob": -5.0586, "norm_logp": -0.0609, "input_len": 486, "output_tokens": 83, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.31, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "519", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how raffles work:\n\n1. Reverse Raffle: In this type of raffle, the numbers on the entry tickets are drawn in reverse order, starting with the last number and ending with the first number. For example, if there are 55 entries, the last number drawn wins the grand prize, followed by the next-to-last number, and so on until the first number. Each entry ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1,000 and two $500 prizes (for the early bird drawing and the first ticket pulled).\n2. 50-50 Draw: This is a raffle held during a single day at a specific event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the event by roving staff with handheld electronic devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool, and 50% of the prize pool is awarded to the winner of a random draw that takes place at the end of the event. The remaining 50% goes to a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n3. Additional Raffles: In addition to the main raffle, there may be additional raffles for drinks and dinner. These raffles are not included in the original ticket price and require separate entry fees. One of these raffles offers the chance to win $10,000!\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win $10,000.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, raffles work as follows:\n\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, participants buy tickets with numbers from 1 to approximately 55. Each ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\n\n2. **50-50 Draw**: The 50-50 draw is a raffle held during a single day at a particular event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium during the event by roaming staff equipped with handheld electronic mobile devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool. 50% of the prize pool will go to the winner of the random draw which occurs towards the end of the event. The remaining 50% will be set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These are included with the ticket price and offer the chance to win $10,000.", "time_sec": 8.806, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 435, 80535, 975, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 45695, 431, 44090, 95518, 758, 264, 9931, 435, 44090, 11, 13026, 3695, 14403, 448, 5109, 504, 220, 16, 311, 13187, 220, 20, 20, 13, 8886, 11727, 7049, 400, 20, 15, 11, 323, 264, 1965, 315, 220, 17, 15, 7775, 41575, 825, 4583, 11727, 5802, 400, 16, 15, 15, 15, 13, 2619, 525, 1378, 400, 20, 15, 15, 37408, 25, 825, 369, 279, 4124, 11958, 13330, 323, 825, 369, 279, 1156, 11727, 13238, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 11992, 95518, 576, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 4038, 374, 264, 435, 44090, 5644, 2337, 264, 3175, 1899, 518, 264, 3953, 1538, 13, 35615, 525, 6088, 311, 51760, 6814, 279, 23889, 2337, 279, 1538, 553, 68253, 5570, 18650, 448, 64949, 14346, 6371, 7611, 13, 576, 19952, 33455, 315, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 14403, 6088, 518, 279, 1538, 728, 1119, 264, 21882, 7314, 13, 220, 20, 15, 4, 315, 279, 21882, 7314, 686, 728, 311, 279, 13632, 315, 279, 4194, 4038, 892, 13666, 6974, 279, 835, 315, 279, 1538, 13, 576, 9664, 220, 20, 15, 4, 686, 387, 738, 15663, 369, 264, 47701, 7428, 11882, 553, 279, 34927, 6335, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 47441, 29873, 8751, 431, 80535, 95518, 4220, 525, 5230, 448, 279, 11727, 3349, 323, 3010, 279, 6012, 311, 3164, 400, 16, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1232, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0248, -0.0004, -0.1392, -0.4383, -0.0018, -