{"id": "644", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, first cut large potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, being careful not to break the long sprouts. Place these chunks on a tray and let them dry out in a cool, dark place overnight. Small, sprouted potatoes can be planted whole. Next, prepare your planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches, removing any large rocks or debris. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut your potatoes into cubes of about 1 to 2 inches, making sure to leave a sprout on each cube. Place these cubes in the trench, sprouted side facing up, spaced about 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the potatoes thoroughly and regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Finally, add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting potato vines. This mulch will help keep the soil cool and maintain a consistent moisture level.", "pred": "1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris.\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\n\n1. Prepare the planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and breaking it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris.\n2. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n3. Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\n4. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.", "time_sec": 5.779, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 31166, 279, 47014, 3082, 553, 13054, 279, 17171, 916, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 14719, 432, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 624, 17, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 624, 18, 13, 19649, 279, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 311, 220, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264, 8151, 411, 389, 1817, 23739, 13, 451, 2382, 649, 18533, 14158, 1265, 614, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 389, 1817, 624, 19, 13, 10771, 279, 54104, 1119, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 53252, 11, 49445, 1817, 23739, 504, 279, 1008, 553, 911, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 13, 29279, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 315, 279, 54104, 374, 12880, 705, 624, 20, 13, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 323, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1397, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0399, -0.002, -0.1586, -0.2185, -0.0005, -0.0006, -0.2556, -0.0002, -0.0015, -0.309, -0.232, -0.0035, -0.0352, -0.5536, -0.0031, -0.0001, -0.9681, -0.0567, -0.4231, -0.0103, -0.3724, -0.0358, -0.1488, -0.0, -0.1205, -0.2148, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0006, -0.