{"id": "670", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each item of inventory when it is sold. This is calculated by taking the average cost of all inventories currently available. This cost fluctuates as inventory prices change. The weighted average cost per unit is determined by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. This cost is then assigned to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. This method is often used when it's impossible to assign specific costs to individual units or when the accounting system isn't sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using this method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "time_sec": 3.189, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1369, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0158, -0.0017, -0.0873, -0.446, -0.2331, -0.0013, -0.0399, -0.0606, -0.0814, -0.0007, -0.1716, -0.0555, -0.0001, -0.0014, -0.0065, -0.0016, -0.0502, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0072, -0.0046, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0343, -0.5074, -0.5285, -0.7621, -0.2016, -0.0022, -0.0455, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.1837, -0.0, -0.0014, -0.0, -1.0889, -0.0578, -0.4483, -0.0161, -0.0104, -0.0231, -0.0083, -0.0597, -0.9729, -0.0016, -0.0001, -0.0158, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0031, -0.0009, -0.0008, -0.0017, -0.1437, -0.0306, -0.0003, -0.0793, -0.8164, -0.0901, -0.021, -0.0863, -0.0002, -0.2323, -0.0006, -0.0005, -0.1171, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.1622, -0.0006, -0.0021, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.001, -0.0029, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3228, -0.354, -0.4229, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0141, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.013, -0.0299, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0139, -0.4863], "cumulative_logprob": -9.8338, "norm_logp": -0.0902, "input_len": 630, "output_tokens": 109, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.22, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "673", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer to the question:\n\nThe weighted average method of costing inventory assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns that average price to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change.\n\nTo calculate the weighted average cost per unit, divide the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. The weighted-average figure can then be used to assign costs to both ending inventory and cost of goods sold. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using the weighted average costing method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\n\nWeighted average costing is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns it to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. This method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "time_sec": 3.239, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 13, 1096, 1714, 4990, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 323, 49912, 432, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 13, 8704, 15444, 7576, 38288, 6292, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 686, 1083, 2297, 13, 1096, 1714, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 14740, 1380, 15444, 3589, 525, 773, 946, 5311, 832, 429, 432, 374, 11997, 311, 9793, 264, 3151, 2783, 311, 458, 3842, 4982, 11, 476, 979, 279, 23168, 1849, 374, 537, 38944, 26779, 311, 3754, 47007, 476, 444, 25997, 15444, 13617, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1369, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0158, -0.0017, -0.0873, -0.446, -0.2331, -0.0013, -0.0399, -0.0606, -0.0814, -0.0007, -0.1716, -0.0555, -0.0001, -0.0014, -0.0065, -0.0016, -0.0502, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0072, -0.0046, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0343, -0.5074, -0.5285, -0.7621, -0.2016, -0.0022, -0.0455, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.1837, -0.0, -0.0014, -0.0, -1.0889, -0.0578, -0.4483, -0.0161, -0.0104, -0.0231, -0.0083, -0.0597, -0.9729, -0.0016, -0.0001, -0.0158, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0031, -0.0009, -0.0008, -0.0017, -0.1437, -0.0306, -0.0003, -0.0793, -0.8164, -0.0901, -0.021, -0.0863, -0.0002, -0.2323, -0.0006, -0.0005, -0.1171, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.1622, -0.0006, -0.0021, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.001, -0.0029, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3228, -0.354, -0.4229, -0.0013, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0141,