2, -0.0001, -0.6396, -0.0, -0.01, -0.0035, -0.0076, -0.0207, -0.3181, -0.1008, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0054, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0691, -0.001, -0.0, -0.6032, -0.0105, -0.0048, -0.0, -0.0331, -0.0173, -0.0001, -0.0238, -0.7883, -0.1803, -0.0017, -0.3004, -0.274, -0.042, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0505, -0.0004, -0.1163, -0.0098, -0.0, -0.2269, -0.1235, -0.027, -0.0027, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0638, -0.0067, -0.0, -0.0099, -0.0018, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.5602, -0.0224], "cumulative_logprob": -42.1595, "norm_logp": -0.1882, "input_len": 439, "output_tokens": 224, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 15.09, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "701", "question": "what do children learn with water play", "gt": "Children learn various skills and concepts through water play. It aids in the development of gross motor skills as they engage in activities like running through sprinklers or filling and pouring water from buckets. Water play also facilitates academic learning in areas such as science and math. Children learn about the concept of floating and sinking, and understand that different objects have different buoyancies. In math, they learn about measurement by determining the amount of water that fits into a bucket, and concepts of more and less by comparing water levels in different buckets. Water play also promotes creativity, allowing children to use their imagination.", "pred": "1. Gross motor skills: Children run through sprinklers, fill and pour water, and engage in physical activities that enhance their gross motor skills.\n2. Academic learning: Water play helps with science by teaching concepts like floating and sinking, and with math by practicing measuring and comparing quantities of water.\n3. Creativity: Children use their imagination and pretend while playing with water, fostering creative thinking and expression.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, children learn several things with water play:\n\n1. Gross motor skills: Children run through sprinklers, fill and pour water, and engage in physical activities that enhance their gross motor skills.\n2. Academic learning: Water play helps with science by teaching concepts like floating and sinking, and with math by practicing measuring and comparing quantities of water.\n3. Creativity: Children use their imagination and pretend while playing with water, fostering creative thinking and expression.", "time_sec": 2.876, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 2841, 3960, 3807, 2513, 448, 3015, 1486, 1