{"id": "80", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "The cause of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) is unknown. However, it is believed to be a result of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, disruption in communication between the brain and the GI tract, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors may include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (either too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food. Symptoms of IBS can include diarrhea, constipation, or alternating between the two, belly pains or cramps that worsen after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, as well as a lot of gas or bloating.", "pred": "The causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea, constipation, and alternating between the two. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\n\nThe causes of IBS are thought to be a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to cause IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "time_sec": 7.598, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 13495, 315, 6216, 22533, 65258, 27339, 320, 3256, 50, 8, 2924, 68293, 11, 733, 48586, 11, 323, 72362, 1948, 279, 1378, 13, 96585, 50166, 476, 1560, 14647, 11, 5990, 304, 279, 4722, 4279, 315, 279, 35417, 11, 429, 633, 10960, 1283, 20969, 323, 2666, 2664, 1283, 264, 65258, 7203, 13, 362, 2696, 315, 6819, 476, 14211, 1095, 382, 785, 11137, 315, 358, 7347, 525, 3381, 311, 387, 264, 10601, 315, 34563, 79211, 320, 28034, 8, 41829, 19029, 11, 7172, 17526, 315, 47031, 5746, 11, 323, 264, 43119, 304, 279, 10535, 1948, 279, 8109, 323, 279, 48509, 41829, 13, 1084, 374, 1083, 11585, 311, 387, 4152, 311, 264, 1372, 315, 9363, 11, 2670, 72581, 304, 279, 79211, 320, 28034, 8, 41829, 3852, 1403, 11, 34563, 22596, 1849, 17289, 11, 7172, 26443, 311, 6646, 11, 323, 3607, 71629, 3020, 13, 31288, 9363, 3381, 311, 5240, 358, 7347, 2924, 34563, 19029, 315, 279, 14889, 323, 2613, 38308, 1543, 320, 36127, 4937, 476, 6301, 11, 476, 2238, 3746, 701, 86652, 47837, 311, 6646, 504, 264, 2480, 65258, 476, 6819, 11, 323, 3607, 73533, 42379, 11, 10767, 8881, 553, 7852, 43125, 315, 69813, 476, 32869, 304, 3607, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0297, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0172, -0.0002, -0.1627, -0.5888, -0.1019, -0.1169, -0.3936, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, 