{"id": "181", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "gt": "Hugging has physiological benefits and can have a similar effect on the body as certain drugs. It can also lead to emotional intimacy and happy relationships. Hugging and touch, even with inanimate objects, can help reduce existential fears and worry about mortality.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, hugging has several psychological effects. It can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Additionally, hugging can reduce worry of mortality and soothe existential fears. The use of empathy and listening skills during hugging can also create a psychological hug, making the person feel like they are receiving a real hug.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, hugging has several psychological effects. It can lead to good relationships, emotional intimacy, and happy marriages. Additionally, hugging can reduce worry of mortality and soothe existential fears. The use of empathy and listening skills during hugging can also create a psychological hug, making the person feel like they are receiving a real hug.", "time_sec": 2.312, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 305, 35268, 702, 3807, 23187, 6239, 13, 1084, 646, 2990, 311, 1661, 11871, 11, 14269, 65164, 11, 323, 6247, 46351, 13, 22406, 11, 305, 35268, 646, 7949, 10955, 315, 28428, 323, 43176, 1782, 66639, 23209, 13, 576, 990, 315, 47351, 323, 14289, 7361, 2337, 305, 35268, 646, 1083, 1855, 264, 23187, 29784, 11, 3259, 279, 1697, 2666, 1075, 807, 525, 12308, 264, 1931, 29784, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0443, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0118, -0.0004, -0.0629, -0.8781, -0.0, -0.4358, -1.0366, -0.0359, -0.0133, -0.814, -1.2167, -0.6573, -1.1722, -0.0004, -1.4443, -0.0011, -0.1844, -0.0047, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.0763, -0.0006, -1.4669, -1.3073, -0.0, -0.6624, -0.0002, -0.2915, -0.9376, -0.5312, -0.8602, -0.0004, -0.687, -0.5512, -0.0622, -0.3189, -0.0002, -0.4744, -1.3369, -0.8395, -0.0, -0.1021, -0.0021, -0.0025, -0.0006, -0.6123, -0.3921, -0.0007, -0.4515, -0.3687, -1.5315, -0.0135, -0.3141, -0.0722, -0.6787, -0.722, -0.0374, -0.9044, -0.0107, -0.4845, -0.0023, -0.0032, -0.1365, -0.1081, -0.775, -0.0958, -0.1567, -0.393], "cumulative_logprob": -26.792, "norm_logp": -0.3721, "input_len": 412, "output_tokens": 72, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.58, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "183", "question": "how to cook a small prime rib roast in oven", "gt": "Before cooking the prime rib roast, let it stand at room temperature for at least 1 hour. Preheat the oven to 375 degrees F. Season the roast with a small amount of salt and lots of fresh ground black pepper. Place the roast on a rack in a pan with the rib side down and the fatty side up. Roast it in the oven for 1 hour. After cooking, you can prepare au jus by placing the roasting pan on the stove over high heat. Add wine and cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring and scraping any brown bits. Add beef stock and cook to reduce the juice by half, which might take about 20 minutes. Add salt and pepper to taste.", "pred": "1. Set the oven to 450°F and allow it to preheat for a minimum of 15 minutes.\n2. Season the roast with only a small amount of salt but lots of fresh ground black pepper. Using any other spices will take away the flavor from the prime rib.\n3. Place the roast on a rack in the pan with the rib side down and the fatty side up.\n4. Roast the roast for 1 hour.\n5. Turn off the oven and let the roast rest for at least 1 hour at room temperature.\n6. While the roast is resting, prepare the au jus by placing the roasting pan on the stove over high heat, adding in the wine, cooking for 8-10 minutes, stirring and scraping any brown bits, adding in the beef stock, cooking and reducing the juice by half (this might take about 20 minutes), and adding salt and pepper to taste.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to cook a small prime rib roast in the oven, follow these steps:\n\n1. Set the oven to 450°F and allow it to preheat for a minimum of 15 minutes.\n2. Season the roast with only a small amount of salt but lots of fresh ground black pepper. Using any other spices will take away the flavor from the prime rib.\n3. Place the roast on a rack in the pan with the rib side down and the fatty side up.\n4. Roast the roast for 1 hour.\n5. Turn off the oven and let the roast rest for at least 1 hour at room temperature.\n6. While the roast is resting, prepare the au jus by placing the roasting pan on the stove over high heat, adding in the wine, cooking for 8-10 minutes, stirring and scraping any brown bits, adding in the beef stock, cooking and reducing the juice by half (this might take about 20 minutes), and adding salt and pepper to taste.", "time_sec": 7.375, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 4296, 264, 2613, 10250, 20131, 57685, 304, 279, 23387, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 2573, 279, 23387, 311, 220, 19, 20, 15, 58472, 323, 2138, 432, 311, 855, 19963, 369, 264, 8028, 315, 220, 16, 20, 4420, 624, 17, 13, 14263, 279, 57685, 448, 1172, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 12021, 714, 10077, 315, 7722, 4910, 3691, 24353, 13, 12091, 894, 1008, 48559, 686, 1896, 3123, 279, 17172, 504, 279, 10250, 20131, 624, 18, 13, 10771, 279, 57685, 389, 264, 29659, 304, 279, 7215, 448, 279, 20131, 3108, 1495, 323, 279, 38985, 3108, 705, 624, 19, 13, 11830, 559, 279, 57685, 369, 220, 16, 6460, 624, 20, 13, 11999, 1007, 279, 23387, 323, 1077, 279, 57685, 2732, 369, 518, 3245, 220, 16, 6460, 518, 3054, 9315, 624, 21, 13, 5976, 279, 57685, 374, 40119, 11, 10549, 279, 7906, 44807, 553, 24047, 279, 926, 14725, 7215, 389, 279, 44015, 916, 1550, 8628, 11, 7842, 304, 279, 13078, 11, 17233, 369, 220, 23, 12, 16, 15, 4420, 11, 53954, 323, 81438, 894, 13876, 9472, 11, 7842, 304, 279, 24317, 5591, 11, 17233, 323, 17719, 279, 22815, 553, 4279, 320, 574, 2578, 1896, 911, 220, 17, 15, 4420, 701, 323, 7842, 12021, 323, 24353, 311, 12656, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1608, -0.0, -0.0, -0.027, -0.0005, -0.1231, -0.3583, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.004, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.073, -0.0, -0.6857, -0.043, -0.0052, -0.0106, -0.3274, -0.0027, -0.0012, -0.8279, -0.0251, -0.0002, -0.0249, -0.0227, -0.1131, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0077, -0.0305, -0.0599, -0.2037, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0038, -0.477, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.1519, -0.0023, -0.0, -0.1271, -0.0004, -0.0339, -0.0061, -0.3673, -0.0002, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2092, -0.0282, -0.0, -0.0054, -0.0008, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.8263, -0.8115, -0.1742, -0.0017, -0.0007, -0.1369, -0.1411, -0.0001, -0.0013, -0.0014, -0.0457, -0.0001, -0.0032, -0.0, -0.3446, -0.0028, -0.0, -0.7712, -0.002, -0.1188, -0.0632, -0.0036, -0.009, -0.0056, -0.2021, -0.1088, -0.1023, -0.0003, -0.0059, -0.0003, -0.0012, -0.0063, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.166, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.1745, -0.0, -0.2666, -0.6284, -0.3857, -0.375, -0.0527, -0.1197, -0.3117, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.1812, -0.0024, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.892, -0.6506, -0.0206, -0.0048, -0.5866, -1.3719, -0.6084, -0.0001, -0.0223, -0.0017, -0.0132, -1.2629, -0.0, -0.0, -0.9178, -0.0026, -0.0, -0.1353, -0.0081, -0.0011, -0.0239, -0.0012, -0.0019, -0.0027, -0.0058, -0.0007, -0.0001, -0.3014, -0.222, -0.0001, -0.0026, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0636, -0.6711, -0.0036, -0.0079, -0.0004, -0.0, -1.1339, -0.0045, -0.6518, -0.2058, -0.0003, -0.2185, -0.3462, -0.0375, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0026, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4384, -0.1317, -0.0106, -0.0016, -0.0043, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0664, -0.1126, -0.0528, -0.0163, -0.0008, -0.0002, -0.036, -0.2148, -0.0333, -0.0006, -0.0084, -0.0022, -0.0078, -0.0003, -0.5254, -0.1196, -0.0378, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0036, -0.0446, -0.1621, -0.0007, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.2471, -0.0218], "cumulative_logprob": -23.8315, "norm_logp": -0.1119, "input_len": 664, "output_tokens": 213, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 74.16, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "189", "question": "how long does it take to make chicken tender in crock pot", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the answer to the question \"How long does it take to make chicken tender in a crock pot?\" is:\n\n6-8 hours on low heat, or 4 hours on high heat if you're short on time.\n\nThe first passage suggests cooking the chicken on low for 6-8 hours, or 1 hour on high and then 5 hours on low. The second passage repeats the same information. The third passage provides additional information that chicken breast will always come out tender as long as there is enough moisture, and suggests cooking it for 4 hours on high if you're short on time.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is 6-8 hours on low heat, or 4 hours on high heat if you're short on time.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, it takes 6 - 8 hours to make chicken tender in a crock pot when cooking on low.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, it takes 6 - 8 hours to make chicken tender in a crock pot when cooking on low.", "time_sec": 0.902, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 432, 4990, 220, 21, 481, 220, 23, 4115, 311, 1281, 16158, 27582, 304, 264, 14093, 377, 3338, 979, 17233, 389, 3347, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0545, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0385, -0.0037, -0.0204, -0.3892, -0.0234, -0.1951, -0.3799, -0.3114, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.2357, -0.2263, -0.0013, -0.0054, -0.0029, -0.3189, -0.0895, -0.0, -0.0132, -0.3152, -0.7961, -0.052, -0.0072, -0.4562, -0.8867], "cumulative_logprob": -4.8229, "norm_logp": -0.1663, "input_len": 633, "output_tokens": 29, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.92, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "193", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some steps for attaching multiple picture frames together:\n\nPassage 1: Table-Stand Grouping\nTo join two or three frames together, you can use identical frames or one larger frame as the middle piece. Place the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect, and then mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides. Attach two small hinges to the marked areas to join the frames together.\n\nPassage 2: Hanging Multiple Pictures Together\nBefore attaching the frames together, test different arrangements of the group of multiple pictures on the floor. Once you have decided on the layout, screw the frames to the backer board, repeating the process for each frame. Attach a hanging wire to the back of the frames and hang them on the wall to enjoy the finished result.\n\nPassage 3: Attaching Frames Together\nTo attach two frames together, place them back to back and mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides. The placement should be the same distance from the bottom edge of each frame to ensure stability when the grouped pictures are placed on a table. Attach two small hinges to the marked areas to join the frames together.\n\nIn conclusion, the recommended method for attaching multiple picture frames together involves using identical or matching frames, marking the hinge placement on the connecting sides, and attaching small hinges to join the frames together. It's also important to test different arrangements of the group of pictures on the floor before attaching the frames together, and to ensure that the frames are securely attached to the backer board before hanging them on the wall.", "pred": "1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding.\n2. For joining two frames, use identical frames.\n3. For joining three frames, choose either three of the same or one which is larger to be the middle frame.\n4. Lay the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect.\n5. Put two frames together back to back and mark the hinge placement.\n6. Ensure the hinge placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure the grouped pictures stand securely on the table top.\n7. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to attach multiple picture frames together, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding.\n2. For joining two frames, use identical frames.\n3. For joining three frames, choose either three of the same or one which is larger to be the middle frame.\n4. Lay the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect.\n5. Put two frames together back to back and mark the hinge placement.\n6. Ensure the hinge placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure the grouped pictures stand securely on the table top.\n7. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.", "time_sec": 4.765, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 15498, 5248, 6802, 14087, 3786, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 8427, 1378, 476, 2326, 14087, 315, 279, 1852, 7579, 65057, 624, 17, 13, 1752, 18169, 1378, 14087, 11, 990, 19516, 14087, 624, 18, 13, 1752, 18169, 2326, 14087, 11, 5157, 2987, 2326, 315, 279, 1852, 476, 825, 892, 374, 8131, 311, 387, 279, 6149, 4034, 624, 19, 13, 43924, 279, 14087, 29388, 1817, 1008, 311, 1490, 421, 498, 1075, 279, 2456, 624, 20, 13, 10224, 1378, 14087, 3786, 1182, 311, 1182, 323, 1868, 279, 75593, 21448, 624, 21, 13, 29279, 279, 75593, 21448, 374, 279, 1852, 6010, 504, 279, 5622, 12822, 315, 1817, 4034, 311, 5978, 279, 40041, 9185, 2498, 51023, 389, 279, 1965, 1909, 624, 22, 13, 48384, 1378, 2613, 79834, 311, 279, 20913, 11067, 315, 279, 14087, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.141, -0.0, -0.