{"id": "199", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused primarily by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci, also known as Streptococcus pyogenes. This is the same bacteria that causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria types, such as staphylococcus, are also associated with the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Prior trauma, such as a cut or scrape, insect bite, burn, or needle puncture wound can serve as a source of infection as well as surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures. In rare cases, fungi can lead to this condition.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. These bacteria can include group A Streptococcus (GAS), staphylococcus, and others. The infections caused by these bacteria are usually mild, but in rare cases, they can lead to a more dangerous infection that can destroy skin, fat, and the tissue covering the muscles within a very short time. This condition is sometimes referred to as flesh-eating bacteria.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. These bacteria can include group A Streptococcus (GAS), staphylococcus, and others. The infections caused by these bacteria are usually mild, but in rare cases, they can lead to a more dangerous infection that can destroy skin, fat, and the tissue covering the muscles within a very short time. This condition is sometimes referred to as flesh-eating bacteria.", "time_sec": 3.848, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 19053, 4640, 4849, 15195, 72, 19435, 374, 8881, 553, 3807, 12829, 315, 23157, 13, 4220, 23157, 646, 2924, 1874, 362, 35672, 417, 509, 91311, 320, 38, 1911, 701, 357, 1342, 88, 1074, 91311, 11, 323, 3800, 13, 576, 28920, 8881, 553, 1493, 23157, 525, 5990, 23034, 11, 714, 304, 8848, 5048, 11, 807, 646, 2990, 311, 264, 803, 11406, 18873, 429, 646, 6921, 6787, 11, 8664, 11, 323, 279, 19847, 18202, 279, 23648, 2878, 264, 1602, 2805, 882, 13, 1096, 2971, 374, 7025, 13862, 311, 438, 24955, 5655, 1095, 23157, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0774, -0.0, -0.0, -0.019, -0.0003, -0.0293, -0.1065, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0954, -0.4135, -0.0246, -0.2142, -0.1883, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.6644, -1.3057, -0.1518, -0.8844, -0.9338, -0.188, -0.0, -0.5927, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.1389, -0.0319, -0.0003, -0.2545, -0.4849, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0078, -0.0027, -0.0193, -0.4053, -0.0151, -1.4934, -0.7861, -0.0395, -0.0, -0.0035, -0.0003, -0.2838, -0.1075, -0.0021, -0.4662, -0.0424, -0.1995, -0.0053, -0.0021, -0.1479, -0.0029, -0.0003, -0.7008, -0.0, -0.1486, -0.3411, -0.0686, -0.796, -0.9349, -0.3691, -0.0674, -0.0077, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0499, -0.0009, -0.0005, -0.0111, -0.0, -0.103, -0.0002, -0.0234, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0292, -1.3115, -0.5202, -0.0464, -0.5049, -0.307, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4803, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -1.0007, -0.4333], "cumulative_logprob": -19.0927, "norm_logp": -0.1948, "input_len": 668, "output_tokens": 98, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.55, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "204", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Chard, also known as Swiss chard, is a vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a flavor that is stronger and more assertive than spinach, and some people find it bitter. Chard can be eaten raw in salads when it's young, but mature leaves and stalks are usually cooked or sautéed. Chard is actually a type of beet that is grown for its leaves rather than its root. It can produce white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. Chard is highly perishable and should be kept in the refrigerator for only 2 to 3 days.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, chard is a leafy green vegetable that is a member of the beet family. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be stored in the refrigerator for 2 to 3 days.", "time_sec": 4.013, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 521, 567, 374, 264, 15933, 88, 6176, 35481, 429, 374, 264, 4462, 315, 279, 48012, 2997, 13, 1084, 702, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 714, 702, 264,