{"id": "211", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom, with all atoms having the same number of protons. Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded, allowing for easy separation of the components using physical methods.", "pred": "1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\n\n1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "time_sec": 4.715, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 34157, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 323, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 624, 17, 13, 13317, 3095, 525, 32055, 23415, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 323, 525, 1083, 10526, 32055, 13, 576, 5424, 304, 264, 23628, 525, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 4157, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 624, 18, 13, 19219, 18513, 525, 264, 6961, 10601, 315, 5424, 476, 31146, 1380, 279, 5479, 315, 279, 20980, 525, 537, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 646, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 13, 19219, 18513, 646, 614, 264, 3890, 18037, 323, 1473, 279, 5888, 315, 862, 74064, 32055, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1444, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0118, -0.0005, -0.1424, -0.4201, -0.1473, -0.0008, -0.0024, -0.002, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2343, -0.2807, -0.0001, -0.0919, -0.3319, -0.0126, -0.2876, -0.7568, -1.0507, -0.0063, -0.1071, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.002, -0.7509, -0.1401, -0.0908, -0.0137, -0.0001, -0.0029, -0.0004, -0.0221, -0.0029, -0.0001, -0.4791, -0.0079, -0.0, -0.0317, -0.0, -0.0089, -0.1718, -0.0473, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.001, -0.5923, -0.021, -0.6132, -0.0223, -0.0008, -0.4598, -0.7456, -0.5915, -0.2004, -0.049, -0.0, -0.0096, -0.0427, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.7492, -1.025, -0.0061, -0.1245, -0.231, -0.0431, -0.0433, -0.1158, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0027, -0.0, -0.0589, -0.5763, -0.0705, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0042, -0.0026, -0.0491, -0.0003, -0.0251, -0.3856, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0139, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0596, -0.824, -0.7021, -0.1326, -0.0382, -0.0008, -0.1607, -0.3983, -0.2245, -0.0001, -0.73, -0.5372, -0.2131, -0.0049, -0.0, -0.0681, -0.1998, -0.0087, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.998, -0.0037, -0.002, -0.4344, -0.298], "cumulative_logprob": -18.7242, "norm_logp": -0.1486, "input_len": 563, "output_tokens": 126, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 59.62, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "213", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the main differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are:\n\n* Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom. They have a fixed composition and cannot be broken down into simpler substances by physical means.\n* Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements that are chemically bonded together. They have a fixed composition and are pure substances.\n* Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. They have a variable composition and can be separated by physical means.\n\nTherefore, the key differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are their composition, bonding, and separability. Elements are homogeneous and have a fixed composition, compounds are heterogeneous and have a fixed composition, and mixtures are heterogeneous and have a variable composition. Additionally, elements and compounds are chemically bonded, while mixtures are not.", "pred": "1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures are as follows:\n\n1. Elements are composed of only one type of atom and are the purest form of a chemical substance.\n2. Compounds are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. The elements in a compound are chemically bonded and cannot be separated by physical means.\n3. Mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and can be separated by physical means. Mixtures can have a variable composition and show the properties of their constituent substances.", "time_sec": 4.607, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 11799, 1948, 5424, 11, 31146, 11, 323, 6514, 18513, 525, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 34157, 525, 23415, 315, 1172, 825, 943, 315, 19124, 323, 525, 279, 10526, 267, 1352, 315, 264, 11483, 19699, 624, 17, 13, 13317, 3095, 525, 32055, 23415, 315, 1378, 476, 803, 2155, 5424, 323, 525, 1083, 10526, 32055, 13, 576, 5424, 304, 264, 23628, 525, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 4157, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 624, 18, 13, 19219, 18513, 525, 264, 6961, 10601, 315, 5424, 476, 31146, 1380, 279, 5479, 315, 279, 20980, 525, 537, 8424, 2673, 69141, 323, 646, 387, 18663, 553, 6961, 3363, 13, 19219, 18513, 646, 614, 264, 3890, 18037, 323, 1473, 279, 5888, 315, 862, 74064, 32055, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1444, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0118, -0.0005, -0.1424, -0.4201, -0.1473, -0.0008, -0.0024, -0.002, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2343, -0.2807, -0.0001, -0.0919, -0.3319, -0.0126, -0.2876, -0.7568, -1.0507, -0.0063, -0.1071, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.002, -0.7509, -0.1401, -0.0908, -0.0137, -0.0001, -0.0029, -0.0004, -0.0221, -0.0029, -0.0001, -0.4791, -0.0079, -0.0, -0.0317, -0.0, -0.0089, -0.1718, -0.0473, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.001, -0.5923, -0.021, -0.6132, -0.0223, -0.0008, -0.4598, -0.7456, -0.5915, -0.2004, -0.049, -0.0, -0.0096, -0.0427, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.7492, -1.025, -0.0061, -0.1245, -0.231, -0.0431, -0.0433, -0.1158, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0027, -0.0, -0.0589, -0.5763, -0.0705, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0011, -0.0042, -0.0026, -0.0491, -0.0003, -0.0251, -0.3856, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0139, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0596, -0.824, -0.7021, -0.1326, -0.0382, -0.0008, -0.1607, -0.3983, -0.2245, -0.0001, -0.73, -0.5372, -0.2131, -0.0049, -0.0, -0.0681, -0.1998, -0.0087, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.998, -0.0037, -0.002, -0.4344, -0.298], "cumulative_logprob": -18.7242, "norm_logp": -0.1486, "input_len": 563, "output_tokens": 126, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.97, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.8, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "215", "question": "how to wash wine glasses properly", "gt": "To wash wine glasses properly, start by using hot water and mild detergent to clean the outside of the glass. If there are stubborn wine residues, use baking soda to dissolve them by mixing a small amount of soda with hot water and letting it sit in the glass for a few minutes. For cleaning the inside of the glass, use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles. Rinse thoroughly with hot water and dry the glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth. To avoid damage, wash one wine glass at a time, use a small amount of detergent only where it's needed, and cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.", "pred": "1. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\n2. After rinsing thoroughly with hot water, dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\n3. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adhering to the glass.\n4. Clean your wine glasses by hand for the safest method. Lay a towel in the sink before filling with hot water. Wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\n5. Use a small amount of detergent and use it only where it's needed -- in the bottom of the glass and around the rim. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\n6. Avoid chips and spots by following these tips.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to wash wine glasses properly, follow these steps:\n\n1. Use a stemware brush with soft-foam bristles to clean the inside of the glass.\n2. After rinsing thoroughly with hot water, dry the wine glasses with a smooth lint-free cloth, such as a microfiber or flour sack towel.\n3. If hot water and mild detergent can't get your glasses clean, use baking soda to remove any stubborn wine residue. Dissolve a small amount of soda with hot water and let it stand in the glass for a few minutes. The soda will dissolve any residual wine adhering to the glass.\n4. Clean your wine glasses by hand for the safest method. Lay a towel in the sink before filling with hot water. Wash one wine glass at a time to avoid damage.\n5. Use a small amount of detergent and use it only where it's needed -- in the bottom of the glass and around the rim. Cradle the bowl of the glass in one hand while washing to protect the stem.\n6. Avoid chips and spots by following these tips.", "time_sec": 8.312, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 11369, 13078, 28147, 10277, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 5443, 264, 19101, 1664, 15625, 448, 8413, 12, 824, 309, 1411, 65943, 311, 4240, 279, 4766, 315, 279, 8991, 624, 17, 13, 4636, 96039, 287, 26364, 448, 4017, 3015, 11, 9058, 279, 13078, 28147, 448, 264, 10876, 57920, 12577, 27292, 11, 1741, 438, 264, 8003, 82945, 476, 19828, 52333, 42613, 624, 18, 13, 1416, 4017, 3015, 323, 23034, 90134, 646, 944, 633, 697, 28147, 4240, 11, 990, 27815, 38862, 311, 4057, 894, 51396, 13078, 48132, 13, 51032, 3948, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 38862, 448, 4017, 3015, 323, 1077, 432, 2498, 304, 279, 8991, 369, 264, 2421, 4420, 13, 576, 38862, 686, 72639, 894, 32147, 13078, 34951, 287, 311, 279, 8991, 624, 19, 13, 9590, 697, 13078, 28147, 553, 1424, 369, 279, 61744, 1714, 13, 43924, 264, 42613, 304, 279, 19309, 1573, 21274, 448, 4017, 3015, 13, 5985, 825, 13078, 8991, 518, 264, 882, 311, 5648, 5557, 624, 20, 13, 5443, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 90134, 323, 990, 432, 1172, 1380, 432, 594, 4362, 1177, 304, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 8991, 323, 2163, 279, 30130, 13, 4553, 71355, 279, 19212, 315, 279, 8991, 304, 825, 1424, 1393, 27686, 311, 6016, 279, 19101, 624, 21, 13, 34006, 23595, 323,