{"id": "226", "question": "how to fix lift valve in toilet", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to fix a lift valve in a toilet:\n\n1. Shut off the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve and any remaining pieces of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n3. Install the new flapper valve and connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm, making sure it is securely fastened.\n4. Open the water valve to fill the tank and flush the toilet to test for correct operation.\n5. Adjust the chain as needed to ensure a proper flush. Cut off any excess chain to prevent tangling.\n\nNote: If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "pred": "1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms.\n3. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n4. Open the water valve to fill the tank.\n5. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\n6. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\n7. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\n8. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle.\n9. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly.\n10. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure.\n11. The other end will hang freely in the air.\n12. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm.\n13. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm.\n14. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\n15. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\n16. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to fix the lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\n\n1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms.\n3. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n4. Open the water valve to fill the tank.\n5. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\n6. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\n7. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\n8. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle.\n9. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly.\n10. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure.\n11. The other end will hang freely in the air.\n12. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm.\n13. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm.\n14. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\n15. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\n16. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.", "time_sec": 10.803, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 5046, 279, 11893, 31430, 304, 264, 26214, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1417, 27440, 279, 3015, 8149, 31430, 311, 279, 26214, 624, 17, 13, 10783, 279, 2310, 1320, 3106, 31430, 553, 63702, 432, 1007, 279, 33639, 11715, 624, 18, 13, 10783, 1128, 748, 2115, 315, 279, 10865, 11893, 8781, 504, 279, 11893, 6773, 624, 19, 13, 5264, 279, 3015, 31430, 311, 5155, 279, 12896, 624, 20, 13, 57626, 279, 26214, 311, 1779, 369, 4396, 5666, 624, 21, 13, 1416, 4362, 11, 1349, 4250, 279, 11893, 8781, 553, 7218, 279, 9317, 12327, 705, 476, 1495, 264, 5625, 315, 7746, 624, 22, 13, 3197, 498, 3129, 19527, 11, 3931, 1007, 279, 13623, 11893, 8781, 311, 5358, 432, 504, 3709, 92841, 624, 23, 13, 22950, 279, 2613, 6437, 835, 315, 279, 26214, 11893, 6773, 1119, 279, 18198, 3705, 624, 24, 13, 1416, 264, 21966, 374, 5230, 448, 697, 949, 11, 21966, 