{"id": "240", "question": "how do crab traps work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a crab trap is a large square metal cage with a system of nets inside that allows crabs to enter through a narrow slit at the top. To set the trap, the ends with lead-ins are placed in the water and the crabs are attracted to the free meal offered by the trap. The Alaska Department of Fish and Game recommends using king crab pots that range from 6 by 6 feet to 8 by 8 feet with heights between 2 and 3 feet. Over time, these mechanisms may disable the trap, preventing it from ghost fishing.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. The crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. The crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.", "time_sec": 4.121, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 59412, 44560, 975, 553, 1667, 264, 1849, 315, 52457, 4766, 264, 3460, 9317, 35360, 1865, 315, 9067, 476, 10063, 9317, 11294, 429, 374, 48580, 448, 9509, 15904, 13, 576, 44560, 525, 6188, 311, 2138, 1560, 3435, 304, 714, 537, 1077, 1105, 700, 13, 576, 59412, 3338, 374, 264, 3460, 9334, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1550, 323, 220, 23, 7541, 6884, 13, 9043, 10335, 614, 2990, 21853, 429, 9210, 1119, 279, 22862, 504, 279, 11067, 323, 5622, 311, 264, 15026, 78030, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1119, 279, 3338, 13, 1096, 78030, 374, 279, 1172, 19809, 1119, 279, 22862, 13, 576, 1560, 3435, 13083, 807, 1969, 45664, 705, 279, 11508, 311, 633, 311, 862, 1910, 15145, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0344, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0159, -0.0012, -0.0715, -0.0229, -0.0167, -0.1003, -0.1398, -0.3201, -0.0317, -0.1357, -0.0159, -0.0061, -0.0079, -0.0114, -0.0138, -0.0074, -0.0002, -0.2648, -0.0025, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.3352, -0.0015, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0188, -0.2337, -0.5133, -0.468, -0.055, -0.016, -0.0081, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.1314, -0.0082, -0.0036, -0.0048, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0841, -0.8743, -0.9532, -0.5617, -0.0921, -0.0873, -0.0035, -0.0025, -0.1191, -0.0023, -0.0072, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0021, -0.0009, -0.0029, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.6367, -0.0304, -0.0004, -0.0265, -0.013, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0069, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0033, -0.0555, -0.0641, -0.0048, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0515, -0.0623, -0.0076, -0.0, -0.0909, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.0009, -0.0057, -0.0036, -0.0001, -0.0494, -0.1651], "cumulative_logprob": -7.1105, "norm_logp": -0.0583, "input_len": 598, "output_tokens": 122, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.61, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "243", "question": "how do crab traps work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how crab traps work:\n\nCrab traps are large square structures made of wire or flat metal mesh, with two ends that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom, leading to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. Crabs are attracted to bait inside the pot and must crawl up the leads to reach it, but once they enter, they cannot escape due to the narrow opening. The pot is designed to allow crabs in but not let them out, making it an effective tool for catching crabs.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"How do crab traps work?\" is: Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage with a narrow entrance that allows crabs to enter but not exit, making it an effective tool for catching crabs.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. The crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. The crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.", "time_sec": 4.189, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 59412, 44560, 975, 553, 1667, 264, 1849, 315, 52457, 4766, 264, 3460, 9317, 35360, 1865, 315, 9067, 476, 10063, 9317, 11294, 429, 374, 48580, 448, 9509, 15904, 13, 576, 44560, 525, 6188, 311, 2138, 1560, 3435, 304, 714, 537, 1077, 1105, 700, 13, 576, 59412, 3338, 374, 264, 3460, 9334, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1550, 323, 220, 23, 7541, 6884, 13, 9043, 10335, 614, 2990, 21853, 429, 9210, 1119, 279, 22862, 504, 279, 11067, 323, 5622, 311, 264, 15026, 78030, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1119, 279, 3338, 13, 1096, 78030, 374, 279, 1172, 19809, 1119, 279, 22862, 13, 576, 1560, 3435, 13083, 807, 1969, 45664, 705, 279, 11508, 311, 633, 311, 862, 1910, 15145, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0344, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0159, -0.0012, -0.0715, -0.0229, -0.0167, -0.1003, -0.1398, -0.3201, -0.0317, -0.1357, -0.0159, -0.0061, -0.0079, -0.0114, -0.0138, -0.0074, -0.0002, -0.2648, -0.0025, -0.0002, -0.0007, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.3352, -0.0015, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0188, -0.2337, -0.5133, -0.468, -0.055, -0.016, -0.0081, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.1314, -0.0082, -0.0036, -0.0048, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0841, -0.8743, -0.9532, -0.5617, -0.0921, -0.0873, -0.0035, -0.0025, -0.1191, -0.0023, -0.0072, -0.0008, -0.0016, -0.0021, -0.0009, -0.0029, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.6367, -0.0304, -0.0004, -0.0265, -0.013, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0004, -0.0069, -0.0008, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0033, -0.0555, -0.0641, -0.0048, -0.001, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0515, -0.0623, -0.0076, -0.0, -0.0909, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.0011, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0008, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.0009, -0.0057, -0.0036, -0.0001, -0.0494, -0.1651], "cumulative_logprob": -7.1105, "norm_logp": -0.0583, "input_len": 598, "output_tokens": 122, "eval_metrics": {"Toke