{"id": "283", "question": "difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, while an adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause is that an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually the verb, and is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, modifies a noun or a pronoun and begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause is that an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually the verb, and is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, as, as if, before, because, if, since, so that, than, though, unless, until, when, where, and while. An adjective clause, on the other hand, modifies a noun or a pronoun and begins with a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, and that) or a subordinate conjunction (when and where).", "time_sec": 4.591, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 458, 993, 22328, 21647, 323, 458, 84492, 21647, 374, 429, 458, 993, 22328, 21647, 82949, 264, 18607, 11, 84492, 11, 476, 2441, 993, 22328, 11, 5990, 279, 18607, 11, 323, 374, 11523, 553, 78163, 31446, 82, 1741, 438, 1283, 11, 7892, 11, 438, 11, 438, 421, 11, 1573, 11, 1576, 11, 421, 11, 2474, 11, 773, 429, 11, 1091, 11, 3498, 11, 7241, 11, 3080, 11, 979, 11, 1380, 11, 323, 1393, 13, 1527, 84492, 21647, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 