{"id": "296", "question": "what are examples of potential and kinetic energy?", "gt": "Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher, which can have a large amount of kinetic energy due to its fast velocity. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, which is the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height, and elastic potential energy. Other activities like a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, rides in an amusement park, and a satellite in orbit also involve potential and kinetic energy.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, examples of potential energy include the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height, such as a satellite in orbit. Examples of kinetic energy include the energy a body possesses by its motion, such as a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, and rides in an amusement park.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, examples of potential energy include the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height, such as a satellite in orbit. Examples of kinetic energy include the energy a body possesses by its motion, such as a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, and rides in an amusement park.", "time_sec": 2.609, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 10295, 315, 4650, 4802, 2924, 279, 4802, 9768, 304, 458, 1633, 438, 279, 1102, 315, 1181, 12140, 2309, 476, 2608, 11, 1741, 438, 264, 23210, 304, 26507, 13, 25311, 315, 70323, 4802, 2924, 279, 4802, 264, 2487, 49226, 553, 1181, 11379, 11, 1741, 438, 264, 1803, 7218, 11, 264, 4935, 20097, 11, 264, 14796, 35512, 11, 458, 33780, 4303, 11, 323, 31227, 304, 458, 61368, 6118, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1738, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0213, -0.0008, -0.287, -0.1624, -0.0021, -0.5331, -0.2522, -0.1986, -0.9362, -0.0677, -0.0076, -0.0068, -0.4667, -0.0024, -0.6132, -0.1445, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0134, -0.0001, -0.0055, -0.0001, -0.3778, -0.9422, -0.0, -0.4163, -1.0835, -0.0039, -0.0003, -0.6908, -0.2443, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.014, -0.2529, -0.0074, -0.1584, -0.0022, -0.0009, -0.0103, -0.0019, -0.0001, -0.0079, -0.0423, -0.0, -0.342, -0.0851, -0.0256, -0.0817, -0.003, -0.0026, -0.0019, -0.0013, -0.7524, -0.0031, -0.0022, -0.0004, -0.3051, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.2121, -0.6469, -0.7734, -0.0033, -0.2227, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0251, -0.1453], "cumulative_logprob": -11.7899, "norm_logp": -0.1593, "input_len": 433, "output_tokens": 74, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.39, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "298", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "gt": "To make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is needed. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot, ensuring hot water is always available. Alternatively, recirculating pumps can be used to rapidly pull hot water from a water heater, while simultaneously sending cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused. These systems can be controlled by the push of a button, a timer, or a motion sensor. However, it should be noted that heat loss from the pipes can occur, requiring the water heater to use more fuel.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is used. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot. This continuous loop of water through the water heater ensures that hot water is always available.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is used. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot. This continuous loop of water through the water heater ensures that hot water is always available.", "time_sec": 2.687, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 1281, 264, 4017, 3015, 42238, 4225, 6334, 11, 264, 4017, 3015, 34755, 13834, 374, 1483, 13, 1096, 13834, 21308, 7010, 3015, 304, 279, 31175, 1182, 311, 279, 3015, 42238, 1526, 264, 470, 1555, 13, 576, 13834, 4225, 23156, 419, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 438, 4362, 311, 2506, 432, 4017, 13, 1096, 19259, 6337, 315, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 25351, 429, 4017, 3015, 374, 2677, 2500, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0833, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0153, -0.0009, -0.1409, -0.892, -0.0102, -0.1536, -0.0067, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.3648, -0.5568, -0.4767, -0.0001, -0.0084, -0.0005, -0.266, -0.1171, -0.0154, -0.1867, -0.0047, -0.0531, -0.0166, -0.0, -0.2456, -0.0008, -0.0006, -0.0019, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0035, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.214, -0.7324, -0.0271, -0.2003, -0.0, -0.0538, -0.0058, -0.0109, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0807, -0.0001, -0.0162, -0.0073, -0.03, -0.0001, -0.3892, -0.1455, -0.1736, -0.0048, -0.0628, -0.0005, -0.0528, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.002, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0747, -0.2833], "cumulative_logprob": -6.1985, "norm_logp": -0.0816, "input_len": 446, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.56, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "300", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "gt": "To make a hot water heater circulate, one can install a recirculation system. The system consists of a pump that pulls hot water from the water heater and simultaneously sends cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused. This ensures that hot water is always available without having to wait for it to circulate naturally. Recirculation systems can be controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is used. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot. This continuous loop of water through the water heater ensures that hot water is always available.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is used. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot. This continuous loop of water through the water heater ensures that hot water is always available.", "time_sec": 2.544, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 1281, 264, 4017, 3015, 42238, 4225, 6334, 11, 264, 4017, 3015, 34755, 13834, 374, 1483, 13, 1096, 13834, 21308, 7010, 3015, 304, 279, 31175, 1182, 311, 279, 3015, 42238, 1526, 264, 470, 1555, 13, 576, 13834, 4225, 23156, 419, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 438, 4362, 311, 2506, 432, 4017, 13, 1096, 19259, 6337, 315, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 25351, 429, 4017, 3015, 374, 2677, 2500, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0833, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0153, -0.0009, -0.1409, -0.892, -0.0102, -0.1536, -0.0067, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.3648, -0.5568, -0.4767, -0.0001, -0.0084, -0.0005, -0.266, -0.1171, -0.0154, -0.1867, -0.0047, -0.0531, -0.0166, -0.0, -0.2456, -0.0008, -0.0006, -0.0019, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0035, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.214, -0.7324, -0.0271, -0.2003, -0.0, -0.0538, -0.0058, -0.0109, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0807, -0.0001, -0.0162, -0.0073, -0.03, -0.0001, -0.3892, -0.1455, -0.1736, -0.0048, -0.0628, -0.0005, -0.0528, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.002, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0747, -0.2833], "cumulative_logprob": -6.1985, "norm_logp": -0.0816, "input_len": 446, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 49.46, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "302", "question": "how to make hot water heater circulate", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to make a hot water heater circulate:\n\nOption 1: Install a recirculating pump. Passage 3 explains that recirculating pumps can rapidly pull hot water from the water heater and send cool water from the hot water lines back to the water heater to be reheated and reused. These pumps can be controlled by a button, timer, or motion sensor.\n\nOption 2: Use a hot water circulation pump. Passage 2 describes how a hot water circulation pump can send cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line, ensuring that hot water is always available when needed.\n\nIt is important to note that running a hot water circulation pump continuously can consume a significant amount of energy, as mentioned in passage 1. Therefore, it may be more cost-effective to only run the pump when needed, such as when you turn on the faucet.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is used. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot. This continuous loop of water through the water heater ensures that hot water is always available.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to make a hot water heater circulate, a hot water circulation pump is used. This pump sends cool water in the pipes back to the water heater through a return line. The pump circulates this water through the water heater as needed to keep it hot. This continuous loop of water through the water heater ensures that hot water is always available.", "time_sec": 2.492, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 1281, 264, 4017, 3015, 42238, 4225, 6334, 11, 264, 4017, 3015, 34755, 13834, 374, 1483, 13, 1096, 13834, 21308, 7010, 3015, 304, 279, 31175, 1182, 311, 279, 3015, 42238, 1526, 264, 470, 1555, 13, 576, 13834, 4225, 23156, 419, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 438, 4362, 311, 2506, 432, 4017, 13, 1096, 19259, 6337, 315, 3015, 1526, 279, 3015, 42238, 25351, 429, 4017, 3015, 374, 2677, 2500, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0833, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0153, -0.0009, -0.1409, -0.892, -0.0102, -0.1536, -0.0067, -0.0001, -0.0007, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.3648, -0.5568, -0.4767, -0.0001, -0.0084, -0.0005, -0.266, -0.1171, -0.0154, -0.1867, -0.0047, -0.0531, -0.0166, -0.0, -0.2456, -0.0008, -0.0006, -0.0019, -0.002, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0035, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.214, -0.7324, -0.0271, -0.2003, -0.0, -0.0538, -0.0058, -0.0109, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0807, -0.0001, -0.0162, -0.0073, -0.03, -0.0001, -0.3892, -0.1455, -0.1736, -0.0048, -0.0628, -0.0005, -0.0528, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.002, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0011, -0.0005, -0.0747, -0.2833], "cumulative_logprob": -6.1985, "norm_logp": -0.0816, "input_len": 446, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 44.74, "GAVIE-Rel": 8.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "304", "question": "what causes watermelons to explode", "gt": "Watermelons can explode due to external pressure, such as when a bunch of rubber bands are placed around the middle of the watermelon, causing potential energy to build up and eventually cause the watermelon to explode. Another cause of watermelon explosions is the buildup of gas inside the watermelon due to rotting. This increases the internal pressure and when the skin of the watermelon can no longer hold the gas inside, it will explode.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, watermelons explode due to the build-up of gas inside the watermelon as it rots. The gas pressure increases over time, eventually becoming too strong for the watermelon's skin to contain. When the skin is no longer able to withstand the pressure, the watermelon explodes, often spraying nearby surfaces with the contents of the watermelon.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, watermelons explode due to the build-up of gas inside the watermelon as it rots. The gas pressure increases over time, eventually becoming too strong for the watermelon's skin to contain. When the skin is no longer able to withstand the pressure, the watermelon explodes, often spraying nearby surfaces with the contents of the watermelon.", "time_sec": 2.477, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 3015, 27127, 2382, 15758, 4152, 311, 279, 1936, 5239, 315, 6819, 4766, 279, 3015, 71118, 438, 432, 926, 2576, 13, 576, 6819, 7262, 12703, 916, 882, 11, 9583, 10454, 2238, 3746, 369, 279, 3015, 71118, 594, 6787, 311, 6644, 13, 3197, 279, 6787, 374, 902, 5021, 2952, 311, 50471, 279, 7262, 11, 279, 3015, 71118, 93913, 11, 3545, 82536, 14046, 26431, 448, 279, 8794, 315, 279, 3015, 71118, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0201, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0157, -0.0012, -0.0383, -0.4004, -0.0097, -0.0, -0.102, -0.0457, -0.0, -0.142, -1.2953, -0.0025, -0.0013, -0.6241, -0.5165, -0.6991, -0.3589, -0.0041, -0.7538, -0.068, -0.6102, -0.0001, -0.2758, -0.6666, -0.4413, -1.1633, -0.3952, -0.7263, -0.0, -0.2994, -1.0457, -0.5111, -0.0664, -0.3166, -0.0034, -0.0, -0.0278, -0.0002, -0.022, -0.002, -0.0101, -0.2665, -0.7294, -0.723, -0.024, -0.1018, -0.8921, -0.06, -0.0, -0.7402, -0.0, -0.4061, -0.0298, -0.1755, -0.0063, -0.1814, -0.0057, -0.0001, -0.2471, -0.2938, -0.4997, -0.1403, -1.1746, -0.0026, -0.0252, -1.0807, -1.0845, -0.5047, -0.0013, -0.3893, -0.0001, -0.0196, -0.1357], "cumulative_logprob": -21.6233, "norm_logp": -0.2845, "input_len": 420, "output_tokens": 76, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.35, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "312", "question": "avg cost to install a second floor in a house", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the average cost to install a second floor in a house can range from $100 to $400 per square foot, depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the average cost to install a second floor in a house can range from $100 to $400 per square foot, depending on complexity, quality of materials, and local labor rates.", "time_sec": 1.455, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 5461, 2783, 311, 4582, 264, 2086, 6422, 304, 264, 3753, 646, 2088, 504, 400, 16, 15, 15, 311, 400, 19, 15, 15, 817, 9334, 4478, 11, 11649, 389, 23094, 11, 4271, 315, 7236, 11, 323, 2205, 9327, 7813, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1543, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0121, -0.0022, -0.022, -0.0322, -0.1608, -0.0005, -0.0037, -0.0158, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0094, -0.0001, -0.0006, -0.4181, -0.2872, -0.1077, -0.0201, -0.1452, -0.003, -0.0003, -0.4778, -0.0037, -0.4578, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0043, -0.0, -0.0, -0.5931, -0.0782, -0.0002, -0.6169, -0.0054, -0.0116, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0069, -0.021, -0.001, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.014, -0.6396], "cumulative_logprob": -4.3286, "norm_logp": -0.0921, "input_len": 669, "output_tokens": 47, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.69, "GAVIE-Rel": 0.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 0.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "320", "question": "what is the effect of carbon footprint", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that the effect of a carbon footprint is a measure of the negative impact of human activities on the environment. The measurement of a carbon footprint typically indicates the amount of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases produced through daily activities. Additionally, reducing one's carbon footprint can have a positive impact on the environment, even if the changes may seem small.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the effect of carbon footprint is to measure the environmental impact of human activities. It quantifies the total amount of greenhouse gases produced by various human activities within a given time frame, typically measured in tons of carbon dioxide (CO2) released into the atmosphere. This measurement helps in understanding the environmental effect of lifestyle choices, such as using energy for heating, consuming food, and driving vehicles. Reducing carbon footprint involves making changes in daily activities to decrease the amount of greenhouse gases produced, which can lead to a significant positive impact on the environment.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the effect of carbon footprint is to measure the environmental impact of human activities. It quantifies the total amount of greenhouse gases produced by various human activities within a given time frame, typically measured in tons of carbon dioxide (CO2) released into the atmosphere. This measurement helps in understanding the environmental effect of lifestyle choices, such as using energy for heating, consuming food, and driving vehicles. Reducing carbon footprint involves making changes in daily activities to decrease the amount of greenhouse gases produced, which can lead to a significant positive impact on the environment.", "time_sec": 4.264, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 2456, 315, 12499, 42872, 374, 311, 6629, 279, 12158, 5421, 315, 3738, 7488, 13, 1084, 10272, 9606, 279, 2790, 3311, 315, 36547, 44512, 8947, 553, 5257, 3738, 7488, 2878, 264, 2661, 882, 4034, 11, 11136, 16878, 304, 19608, 315, 12499, 39489, 320, 8281, 17, 8, 5880, 1119, 279, 16566, 13, 1096, 18662, 8609, 304, 8660, 279, 12158, 2456, 315, 18899, 11454, 11, 1741, 438, 1667, 4802, 369, 23579, 11, 34108, 3607, 11, 323, 9842, 11474, 13, 3731, 58044, 12499, 42872, 17601, 3259, 4344, 304, 7298, 7488, 311, 18472, 279, 3311, 315, 36547, 44512, 8947, 11, 892, 646, 2990, 311, 264, 5089, 6785, 5421, 389, 279, 4573, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.3989, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0172, -0.002, -0.0402, -0.3364, -0.3064, -0.0001, -0.6011, -0.0005, -0.4391, -0.7141, -0.0231, -0.0025, -0.1113, -0.0005, -0.0038, -1.2603, -0.0022, -0.5089, -0.6042, -0.8492, -0.0035, -0.0046, -0.7302, -0.0289, -0.0, -0.0018, -0.0001, -0.4609, -0.4343, -0.1247, -0.1865, -0.018, -0.1409, -0.0, -0.0306, -0.0139, -0.0018, -0.1531, -0.3359, -0.1477, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.0009, -0.0552, -0.0, -0.034, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.1547, -0.0052, -0.0011, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0104, -1.1046, -0.3962, -0.3111, -0.4789, -0.1229, -0.1769, -0.4079, -0.764, -0.0014, -1.0193, -0.1976, -0.6725, -0.5226, -0.0, -1.1367, -0.6327, -0.9549, -0.04, -0.774, -0.8359, -0.1076, -0.2238, -0.0844, -0.2614, -0.3954, -0.1863, -1.2794, -0.0001, -1.1299, -0.0167, -0.7176, -0.7066, -0.3802, -0.4895, -0.6996, -0.125, -0.1862, -0.6729, -0.1128, -0.513, -0.0, -0.3026, -0.0037, -0.236, -0.3187, -0.5858, -0.034, -1.0794, -0.0, -0.6295, -0.6835, -0.6503, -0.183, -0.002, -0.0108, -0.0002, -0.0824, -0.228], "cumulative_logprob": -33.1706, "norm_logp": -0.2884, "input_len": 515, "output_tokens": 115, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.85, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "324", "question": "how well do patterned rollers work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's what we can conclude about how well patterned rollers work:\n\nPassage 1 suggests that homemade texture or decorative paint rollers can work better than commercial ones for certain faux finishes because they can create a random pattern of texture.\n\nPassage 2 provides a personal testimonial about the effectiveness of the Dermaroller for improving the appearance of skin. The writer claims that it makes their skin look younger and healthier, and that it works best when used with serums or moisturizers. However, there is no direct mention of patterned rollers in this passage.\n\nPassage 3 provides instructions for creating a basic texture roller using an old roller pad with clumped tufts and random patterns created by stretching and twisting rubber bands. This passage suggests that patterned rollers can create a textured finish on walls, but there is no information on how effective they are or how well they work.\n\nTherefore, based on the given passages, it is difficult to say definitively how well patterned rollers work. Passage 1 suggests that they may be effective for certain applications, while passage 2 provides a personal endorsement of a specific product (Dermaroller) for improving skin appearance. Passage 3 provides instructions for creating a basic texture roller, but does not provide any information on its effectiveness. Therefore, the answer to the original question is: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, patterned rollers can work well, but the effectiveness may vary depending on the type of roller and the user's skin type. For example, the Dermaroller worked well for the user on their face, but caused redness on their neck and chest. The Basic Texture Roller, on the other hand, can be effective for applying texture to walls, but requires careful adjustment of the rubber bands to maintain the desired pattern.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, patterned rollers can work well, but the effectiveness may vary depending on the type of roller and the user's skin type. For example, the Dermaroller worked well for the user on their face, but caused redness on their neck and chest. The Basic Texture Roller, on the other hand, can be effective for applying texture to walls, but requires careful adjustment of the rubber bands to maintain the desired pattern.", "time_sec": 3.104, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 5383, 291, 88613, 646, 975, 1632, 11, 714, 279, 26279, 1231, 13289, 11649, 389, 279, 943, 315, 28451, 323, 279, 1196, 594, 6787, 943, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 279, 75408, 277, 69396, 6439, 1632, 369, 279, 1196, 389, 862, 3579, 11, 714, 8881, 2518, 2090, 389, 862, 12975, 323, 15138, 13, 576, 14625, 18757, 44542, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 646, 387, 7373, 369, 18950, 10434, 311, 14285, 11, 714, 7460, 16585, 27075, 315, 279, 22674, 20892, 311, 10306, 279, 12685, 5383, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0424, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0207, -0.002, -0.0337, -0.4868, -0.0, -0.0404, -1.0396, -0.1918, -0.4596, -0.8637, -0.9955, -1.1549, -0.1398, -0.9802, -0.1452, -0.5715, -0.0, -0.1108, -0.9794, -0.2587, -0.7691, -0.1651, -0.138, -1.6166, -0.0675, -0.4738, -0.7533, -0.1406, -0.8716, -0.2989, -0.0018, -0.5921, -0.1029, -0.0001, -0.0009, -1.8423, -0.6618, -0.197, -0.5143, -0.5635, -0.6486, -0.1791, -0.0025, -0.6508, -0.104, -0.8433, -0.1222, -0.0002, -0.1662, -0.1092, -0.001, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.7787, -1.0659, -0.4395, -0.0011, -0.0003, -0.8101, -0.4358, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.7105, -0.9407, -1.4267, -1.0094, -0.6327, -1.2399, -0.4677, -0.0108, -0.4651, -0.4894, -0.8257, -1.3512, -0.5768, -0.1225, -0.43, -0.0199, -0.0016, -0.7163, -0.1402, -0.2221, -0.5344, -0.1257, -0.022, -0.5667], "cumulative_logprob": -37.6953, "norm_logp": -0.4142, "input_len": 530, "output_tokens": 91, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 3.3, "GAVIE-Acc": 2.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "329", "question": "how to boil potatoes for easy peeling", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how to boil potatoes for easy peeling:\n\nPassage 1 recommends boiling the potatoes until they are tender, then transferring them to an ice water bath to stop the cooking process. After cooling, the skin can be easily peeled off. This method takes 15 minutes to cook the potatoes and 5 seconds to peel each one.\n\nPassage 2 suggests peeling the potatoes before cooking, as their skin can be tough and papery. However, no specific instructions are provided for peeling.\n\nPassage 3 provides a step-by-step guide for peeling cooked potatoes. The method involves scoring the potatoes all the way around the center, boiling them for about 15 minutes, then placing them in an ice water bath to cool. Once cooled, the skin can be easily removed. This method takes about 30 minutes total time.\n\nTherefore, based on the provided passages, the best way to boil potatoes for easy peeling is to boil them until tender, then transfer them to an ice water bath to stop the cooking process and make the skin easier to remove. Scoring the potatoes before boiling can also help loosen the skin for easier peeling.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to boil potatoes for easy peeling, follow these steps:\n1. Bring a pot of water to a boil.\n2. With a knife, score your potatoes all the way around the center.\n3. Place your potatoes in the pot and boil for about 15 minutes or so.\n4. Prepare a bowl of ice water and place your potatoes in the water.\n5. Swish it around for a few seconds just enough so that it's cool enough to handle.\n6. Remove your potato from the water and remove the skin.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to boil potatoes for easy peeling, follow these steps:\n1. Bring a pot of water to a boil.\n2. With a knife, score your potatoes all the way around the center.\n3. Place your potatoes in the pot and boil for about 15 minutes or so.\n4. Prepare a bowl of ice water and place your potatoes in the water.\n5. Swish it around for a few seconds just enough so that it's cool enough to handle.\n6. Remove your potato from the water and remove the skin.", "time_sec": 4.578, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 43690, 34167, 369, 4135, 1051, 20058, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 510, 16, 13, 39124, 264, 3338, 315, 3015, 311, 264, 43690, 624, 17, 13, 3085, 264, 21430, 11, 5456, 697, 34167, 678, 279, 1616, 2163, 279, 4126, 624, 18, 13, 10771, 697, 34167, 304, 279, 3338, 323, 43690, 369, 911, 220, 16, 20, 4420, 476, 773, 624, 19, 13, 31166, 264, 19212, 315, 9853, 3015, 323, 1992, 697, 34167, 304, 279, 3015, 624, 20, 13, 4492, 812, 432, 2163, 369, 264, 2421, 6486, 1101, 3322, 773, 429, 432, 594, 7010, 3322, 311, 3705, 624, 21, 13, 10783, 697, 38734, 504, 279, 3015, 323, 4057, 279, 6787, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1844, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0207, -0.0022, -0.14, -0.517, -0.0443, -0.0004, -0.0149, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2847, -0.0604, -0.0075, -0.0039, -0.7773, -0.0797, -0.0063, -0.1874, -0.0015, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0011, -0.0004, -0.0407, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.582, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0002, -0.0028, -0.0396, -0.0007, -0.0016, -0.0025, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0023, -0.0003, -0.0159, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0208, -0.0101, -0.0003, -0.0002, -0.015, -0.0034, -0.0068, -0.0268, -0.0076, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.1822, -0.0209, -0.0189, -0.0078, -0.0, -0.0021, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.1504, -0.0043, -0.0047, -0.0023, -0.0002, -0.0042, -0.0009, -0.0505, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0015, -0.0002, -0.014, -0.0, -0.0022, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0061, -0.0001, -0.0016, -0.0006, -0.0003, -0.4764, -0.0004, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0165, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.0054, -0.6459, -0.4811, -0.0, -0.0008, -0.0226, -0.0197, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.1261, -0.0043], "cumulative_logprob": -5.3952, "norm_logp": -0.0469, "input_len": 512, "output_tokens": 115, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.44, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "332", "question": "how to get rid of ticks from dogs", "gt": "To get rid of ticks from dogs, first, check common tick hiding places such as inside and behind the dog's ears and around the eyes. Once you locate a tick, use a pair of tweezers to grasp the tick's head where it attaches to the skin. If you plan to use your fingers, wear gloves. Pull on the tick gently and steadily to avoid leaving part of the tick's mouth behind, which can cause an infection. Regular use of tick control products can also prevent ticks from attaching to your dog. Your veterinarian can advise you about the best product for your dog and your situation. Flea and tick shampoos can kill the ticks that are already on your pet.", "pred": "Additionally, to prevent ticks from attaching to your dog, it is recommended to use tick control products regularly. Your veterinarian can advise you about the best product for your dog and your situation, considering the diseases common in your area.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to get rid of ticks from dogs, you should follow these steps:\n\n1. Check inside and behind the dog's ears and around its eyes, as these are favorite tick hiding places.\n2. Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin.\n3. Pull on the tick gently and steadily to remove it.\n4. Wear gloves if you plan to use your fingers to remove the tick.\n5. If you yank the tick away too quickly, you might leave part of the tick's mouth behind, which can cause an infection.\n\nAdditionally, to prevent ticks from attaching to your dog, it is recommended to use tick control products regularly. Your veterinarian can advise you about the best product for your dog and your situation, considering the diseases common in your area.", "time_sec": 6.851, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 633, 9279, 315, 29