{"id": "443", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines in several ways:\n\n1. Fire helps to clear the way for new growth by consuming dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, creating a clean slate for seed germination and seedling establishment (Passage 2).\n2. Jack pine seeds are adapted to fire, as they are stored in serotinous cones that only open after a fire has melted the resin holding them closed. This allows the seeds to disperse and establish new populations (Passage 2).\n3. Fire can create a favorable environment for jack pine and aspen regrowth by reducing competition from other tree species, such as spruce, which have better access to sunlight while shading the other trees (Passage 3).\n\nTherefore, forest fires can benefit jack pines by providing a means of seed dispersal and creating conditions that promote their growth and regeneration.", "pred": "1. Fire consumes dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, clearing the way for new growth.\n2. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonous (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out.\n3. In the Canadian boreal forest, aspen and jack pine are the most important 'pioneer species'. These species can regenerate after a fire, as they are more fire-tolerant and can begin to proliferate again after another fire comes and disturbs the cycle.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines in several ways:\n\n1. Fire consumes dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, clearing the way for new growth.\n2. Some species, such as the jack pine, rely on fire to spread their seeds. The jack pine produces seratonous (resin-filled) cones that are very durable. The cones remain dormant until a fire occurs and melts the resin. Then the cones pop open and the seeds fall or blow out.\n3. In the Canadian boreal forest, aspen and jack pine are the most important 'pioneer species'. These species can regenerate after a fire, as they are more fire-tolerant and can begin to proliferate again after another fire comes and disturbs the cycle.", "time_sec": 6.121, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 13638, 26084, 525, 23699, 311, 390, 98813, 1075, 25072, 281, 1543, 304, 3807, 5510, 1447, 16, 13, 6647, 59506, 5593, 11, 1622, 17270, 53732, 389, 279, 13638, 6422, 11, 32750, 279, 1616, 369, 501, 6513, 624, 17, 13, 4329, 9419, 11, 1741, 438, 279, 25072, 33597, 11, 17188, 389, 3940, 311, 8865, 862, 19056, 13, 576, 25072, 33597, 18644, 1420, 23535, 782, 320, 416, 258, 43418, 8, 86892, 429, 525, 1602, 26128, 13, 576, 86892, 7146, 88360, 3080, 264, 3940, 13666, 323, 98091, 279, 53514, 13, 5005, 279, 86892, 2420, 1787, 323, 279, 19056, 4399, 476, 12244, 700, 624, 18, 13, 758, 279, 11888, 38686, 278, 13638, 11, 438, 2752, 323, 25072, 33597, 525, 279, 1429, 2989, 364, 79, 55398, 9419, 4427, 4220, 9419, 646, 79451, 1283, 264, 3940, 11, 438, 807, 525, 803, 3940, 2385, 22072, 517, 323, 646, 3161, 311, 41936, 349, 1549, 1283, 2441, 3940, 4041, 323, 25343, 1279, 279, 10775, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1957, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0197, -0.0014, -0.076, -0.0185, -0.0002, -0.1632, -0.0002, -0.0005, -0.0044, -0.0012, -0.0041, -0.0225, -0.0054, -0.0, -0.2964, -0.5098, -0.0016, -0.4729, -0.0173, -0.0003, -0.74, -1.6153, -0.0043, -0.0369, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.8875, -0.0001, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0078, -0.3064, -0.0812, -0.021, -0.0002, -0.0008, -0.0017, -0.8979, -0.0034, -0.0, -1.3685, -0.3992, -0.117, -0.1997, -0.0, -0.1447, -0.0004, -0.001, -0.0047, -0.0593, -0.0001, -0.0031, -0.0321, -0.0153, -0.0014, -0.000