{"id": "451", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression created by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava empties, causing the overlying rocks to collapse. It is formed by the collapsing of the ground. On the other hand, a crater is a bowl-shaped structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for the eruption of magma and lava. It is formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large, circular depression.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. Craters are caused by impacts from meteorites and can range in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across. In contrast, calderas are formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large, circular depression.", "time_sec": 2.394, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 4553, 11535, 525, 8881, 553, 24905, 504, 41042, 3611, 323, 646, 2088, 304, 1379, 504, 1602, 2613, 311, 3807, 7739, 40568, 3941, 13, 758, 12872, 11, 1620, 1107, 300, 525, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 12942, 304, 264, 3460, 11, 26931, 18210, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1247, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0219, -0.0009, -0.057, -0.0623, -0.0046, -0.0, -0.4381, -0.3337, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.202, -0.6938, -0.005, -0.003, -0.0, -0.0563, -0.7258, -0.0006, -0.0348, -0.1959, -0.0, -0.0117, -0.0002, -0.0