{"id": "551", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how a transistor works:\n\n1. A transistor functions as both an amplifier and a switch.\n2. The transistor's design allows it to control a larger supply of electricity with a small amount of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water.\n3. The base of the transistor pulls electrons from the emitter, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.\n4. The transistor can take in a tiny electric current at one end (the input current) and produce a much bigger electric current at the other end (the output current) when it works as an amplifier.\n5. The holes in the base of the transistor act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector when the transistor is in its off state.\n6. When the electrons and holes move across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, the transistor begins to work.\n7. The collector current is approximately beta (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, and it is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications.\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a miniature electronic component that can function as an amplifier or a switch. It works by using a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a much larger supply of electricity, similar to turning a valve to control a supply of water. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a tiny electric current at one end (an input current) and produces a much bigger electric current (an output current) at the other. Transistors can also work as switches, where the holes in the base act like a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector while the transistor is in its off state. A transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon.", "time_sec": 5.242, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 429, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 1084, 4278, 553, 1667, 264, 2613, 3311, 315, 17728, 311, 2524, 264, 18126, 389, 264, 1753, 8131, 8149, 315, 17728, 11, 4428, 311, 13054, 264, 31430, 311, 2524, 264, 8149, 315, 3015, 13, 3197, 3238, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 13673, 9072, 1482, 518, 825, 835, 320, 276, 1946, 1482, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 11243, 9072, 1482, 320, 276, 2550, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 646, 1083, 975, 438, 31167, 11, 1380, 279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 1075, 264, 22103, 11, 26160, 894, 5089, 1482, 6396, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 1393, 279, 97941, 374, 304, 1181, 1007, 1584, 13, 362, 97941, 4278, 979, 279, 56578, 323, 279, 19766, 1191, 7218, 3941, 279, 1378, 48241, 82, 1948, 279, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1656, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0147,